## Motivation

The field of scientific peer review is in a period of transition and uncertainty. This transition involves a shifting research paradigm where artificial intelligence (AI) is becoming increasingly involved in scientific production. It also involves a crisis in natural sciences, computer sciences, social sciences, and management sciences, due to to a lack of peer reviewers. This raises critical questions about the effectiveness of current methods in ensuring the quality of peer reviews and the potential for AI to assist in this process. The paper aims to address these questions by proposing a competitive preference optimization approach to improve the quality of AI-assisted scientific reviewers (AI scientists). The motivation stems from the need to enhance the reliability and effectiveness of AI-assisted peer reviews, particularly in the context of the rapid and continuous evolution of large language models (LLMs). The paper argues that existing methods for preference optimization are insufficient for this task, as they do not account for the diverse and often conflicting roles of reviewers. The authors propose a novel approach that incorporates a combination of different model dynamics and multi-agent reinforcement learning to overcome these limitations and improve the performance of AI scientists.

## Main Idea

The paper introduces a multi-agent competitive optimization approach that combines different model dynamics and multi-agent reinforcement learning to improve the effectiveness of AI-assisted scientific reviewers (AI scientists). The key innovation is a dual-agent architecture, where a Screening Agent selects the best papers and a Revising Agent provides detailed comments for each paper. This approach outperforms previous methods, including iterative reward optimization, in terms of the quality of reviews and the accuracy of paper rankings. The paper demonstrates that AI scientists can significantly improve the quality of paper reviews and provide insights into which types of paper ideas are favored by the scientific community.

