\title{Improving AI Scientists through Multi-Agent Competitive Preference Optimization}

\begin{abstract}
AI-assisted scientific reviewers (AI scientists), have seen significant progress. Their role becomes crucial as almost 250,000 peer reviewers are needed every year just for computer science research. However, it remains unclear whether current methods like iterative reward optimization can effectively improve the quality of AI scientists. This paper introduces a multi-agent competitive optimization approach that combines diverse model dynamics and multi-agent reinforcement learning. Each paper is reviewed by a screening agent that decides whether to review in-depth, and a revising agent that makes detailed comments. These agents compete in evaluating the same paper with different criteria. Our approach outperforms previous methods, including iterative reward optimization, in terms of the quality of reviews and the accuracy of paper rankings. The experiments show that our method can significantly improve the quality of paper reviews, producing reviews as good as those of a professor-level expert. It can also reveal paper characteristics associated with positive and negative feedback, providing insights into how paper ideas are favored by the scientific community.
\end{abstract}

