\section{Experiments}


In this section, we explore the capability of AI scientists and whether they can outperform AI-assisted paper reviews from GPT-4-Turbo. We show that our method \ours significantly improves the review quality of AI scientists and experts in paper rating and commenting. In addition, the AI scientists achieve the accuracy of paper acceptance decisions as those of experts, which demonstrates the paper quality of AI scientists is as good as that of experts. 

\looseness=-1
We compare the quality of AI scientists with a human expert baseline. We also evaluate the accuracy of paper acceptance decisions by AI scientists in comparison to that of experts. In the experiments, we show that \ours achieves the highest paper rating quality among different LLMs and in comparison to existing optimization methods such as \textit{reward modeling} \citep{NEURIPS2023_91f18a12}, \textit{self-verification} \citep{weng-etal-2023-large}, \textit{iterative preference optimization} \citep{pang2024iterative}, and \textit{reward learning} \citep{lambert2024rewardbench}. We do ablation studies on different prompt designs and different MARL training methods.

\subsection{Empirical Results in Paper Rating}
\label{sec:pe Review Score}

\input{tables/pe_table}

\textbf{Competing with experts in paper rating.} In Table~\ref{tab:pe_table} and Table~\ref{tab:pe_heatmap}, we compare the rating quality of \ours in paper rating. 
In Table \ref{tab:pe_table}, we compare our method with GPT-4-Turbo \citep{reid2024gemini}, GPT-3.5-Turbo (ChatGPT) \citep{achiam2023gpt}, GPT-4 single-round \citep{NEURIPS2020_6b493230}, and experts. From Table \ref{tab:pe_heatmap}, we find that our method significantly improves the overall quality of reviews by up to 21.5\% in the paper rating scenario compared to GPT-4-Turbo, outperforming both IPO-GPT and IPO-GPT-3.5-Turbo. GPT-4 single-round is a baseline method using GPT-4 to generate a one-round paper rating, given the prompt that includes expert reviews. In Table \ref{tab:pe_table}, we show the \textit{Improvement over GPT-4-Turbo}, which allows the readers to understand the relative improvement. Moreover, GPT-3.5-Turbo has a low paper quality due to low-quality reviews in the in-context examples.

In Table \ref{tab:accuracy_of_paper_acceptance_decision}, we study the accuracy of the paper acceptance decisions of \ours. The input is the paper abstract, and the output is the paper rating (accept, undecided, or reject). This experiment uses the same NIPS dataset in \S \ref{sec:eval_setup}. In this paper review experiment, we simulate a two-round paper review by considering two runs with the same prompt but different initial random seeds. The second-round paper review is used as ground truth. In the same table, we show that \ours significantly improves the accuracy of GPT-4-Turbo in predicting positive decisions. Specifically, \ours improves GPT-4-Turbo by 10.8\% accuracy in the first round and overall 8.9\% accuracy in the second round.

\input{tables/accuracy_of_paper_acceptance_decision}
\textbf{Outperforming other LLMs in paper rating.} In Table \ref{tab:accuracy_of_paper_acceptance_decision}, Table \ref{tab:pe_table}, we show that \ours outperforms other LLMs in the paper rating scenario. In paper rating experiments, our method achieves 73.4\% in overall paper quality, outperforming GPT-4-Turbo (61.9\%), GPT-3.5-Turbo (54.6\%), GPT-4 single round (58.0\%), and IPO-GPT. In addition, we find that GPT-4-Turbo tends to assign low scores to rejected papers, while GPT-3.5-Turbo provides high scores. This difference stems from the optimization method and the number of in-context examples. GPT-3.5-Turbo uses the reward model in RLHF during the training while GPT-4-Turbo is not. Notably, GPT-4-Turbo uses more in-context examples than GPT-3.5-Turbo (total: 8 vs. 4). 
