Peer review in scientific communities plays a crucial role in advancing research progress and establishing community-wide knowledge standards \citep{smith2006peer,boughton2018research}. However, the field of peer review is currently in a period of transition and uncertainty. 
On the one hand, the rapid and continuous evolution of large language models (LLMs) \citep{achiam2023gpt, reid2024gemini} has led to the shifting of the research paradigm, where AI is increasingly involved in scientific research \citep{langley1987scientific,wang2023scientific,liang2024mapping}. 
This shift raises critical questions about the effectiveness of current methods in ensuring the quality of peer reviews. 
To address the challenge of a lack of peer reviewers in natural sciences, computer sciences, social sciences, and management sciences, AI has been used in the peer review process to assist experts in providing reviews \citep{hosseini2023fighting}. 
On the other hand, as the number of research papers continues to grow, the demand for peer reviewers is increasing. According to the Open Review platform \footnote{The Open Review platform manages roughly 40\% of AI papers, \url{https://opreview.net/}}, it takes about 400,000 paper reviews per year, but only about 250,000 experts are available to provide these reviews in the computer science field alone. To mitigate the crisis, AI scientists, which are AI agents that emulate the behaviors of human researchers, have emerged \citep{ai4science2023impact, lu2024ai}. These AI agents are capable of automatically summarizing scientific papers \citep{collins2017supervised}, providing comments \citep{liu2024towards}, and offering high-quality suggestions for experiments \citep{jumper2021highly, romera2024mathematical, wang2023scimon, merchant2023scaling, pyzer2022accelerating, hayes2024simulating}. In the peer review process, an AI scientist can provide a well-written review and accurate recommendations for paper decisions by carefully reading papers and providing constructive feedback \citep{liang2024can, li2024ai4r, zhu2024locking}. 

In this paper, we introduce a competitive preference optimization approach to improve the quality of AI scientists and their ability to assist in scientific paper reviews. We establish an environment where AI scientists can compete in reviewing the same research paper, inspired by the role plays in \textit{seminar peer review} \citep{oberg2022teaching}. Each team of AI scientists is randomly assigned a paper to review. These reviews are then summarized into natural language sentences, which serve as optimization objectives for a reward model. The goal of the reward function is to assign higher scores to high-quality reviews of papers with a greater likelihood of acceptance. In training, preference optimization is employed to maximize the reward model for each type of AI scientist. 
Our key contributions involve the exploration and improvement of AI scientists through competitive preference optimization as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:methods_overview} (a). We mainly investigate two scientific paper review scenarios: (1) \textit{Rating-based paper review} (also called \textit{single-round paper rating}): making ratings for \textbf{accept, reject, or undecided}; (2) \textit{Comment-based paper review}: making comments on \textbf{accept, reject, or undecided}. Our well-designed experiments answer the following questions: 

