In summary, our method is inspired by the \textit{seminar peer review} settings, involving different experts who provide individual reviews for a paper. The goal is to use natural language feedback from AI scientists to optimize their performance. 
However, a single optimization objective may not suffice, as different experts often have varying perspectives on paper quality. Our key insight is to train AI scientists through competitive preference optimization, involving multiple agents in the same team. 
These agents compete by assigning different scores to the same paper, ensuring a diverse range of paper reviews in optimization. We assume that the diversity of AI scientists will benefit the reward model to maximize the quality of reviews in the preference optimization. We show that our method enhances the accuracy of AI scientists in evaluating paper quality and provides insights into accepted papers by leveraging diverse perspectives.




\section{Problem Setting}


We introduce \ours as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:method_a}. \ours consists of two agents: an evaluation agent and a screening agent. Each agent is designed to imitate different experts' perspectives on paper quality. Each paper is first evaluated by the evaluation agent, who offers detailed comments or scores. It's worth noting that a high score or positive comments do not necessarily indicate acceptance. The second agent, the screening agent, then decides whether the assigned reviewer should accept or reject the paper after viewing the reviews of the evaluation agent. This screens out reasonable comments or scores on rejected papers. 

The input\footnote{A more detailed version of the procedure is shown in Figure \ref{fig:prompt}.} is denoted as $\mathbf{x}$, the output of the evaluation agent as $\mathbf{y}$, and the screening agent's output is denoted as $\mathbf{z}$. We consider two scenarios for $\mathbf{y}$ and $\mathbf{z}$:
\begin{compactitem}
    \item \textbf{Rating-based paper review (single-round paper rating)}: The evaluation agent expresses an opinion ($\mathbf{y}$) on whether to accept or reject the paper, after which the screening agent ($\mathbf{z}$) outputs 'Yes', 'No', or 'Undecided'. 

    \item \textbf{Comment-based paper review (multi-round paper commenting)}: The evaluation agent provides an open-ended comment on whether to accept or reject the paper ($\mathbf{y}$). Following this, the screening agent ($\mathbf{z}$) determines whether the paper is accepted or rejected based on these comments.
\end{compactitem}



\section{Methodology}

To solve this problem we propose \ours with competitive preference optimization. The optimization objective is to increase the accuracy of the AI scientists in paper rating and commenting. The core idea behind the optimization is to maximize ``rewards'', a measure of quality, for papers that are more likely to be accepted by the reviewer. \ours consists of training a screening agent and an evaluation agent for each paper. The screening agent is designed to imitate the decision-making process of human reviewers and to decide whether to accept or reject a paper after viewing detailed reviews from the evaluation agent.


\subsection{Reward Modeling for a Team of AI Scientists}

We train a series of screening agents and evaluation agents for multiple papers in a round-robin manner. To maximize reward, we optimize the screening agent so that it is more likely to accept high-quality papers and reject low-quality papers. At the same time, we optimize the evaluation agent to make high-quality reviews that will help the screening agent accept the paper. In this formulation, paper quality and the quality of reviews are intertwined: the aim of paper review is to discover new contributions, which usually require experts to make new perspectives \citep{oberg2022teaching}, thus both paper summaries and reviews need to be of high quality to convince reviewers. 

To maximize review quality and paper quality simultaneously, we assign different criteria to reward the outputs of different AI scientists as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:reward (a)}. For simplicity, let's consider that each team has one screening agent and one evaluation agent. The total reward is defined as 
\begin{align}
    r_{\text{total}} = \alpha \cdot r_{\text{screening}} + \beta \cdot r_{\text{evaluation}},
\end{align}
where $r_{\text{screening}}$ and $r_{\text{evaluation}}$ are the scores of the screening agent and the evaluation agent, respectively, and $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are the weights. The scores are calculated by the following procedure:

\begin{compactitem}
    \item (Screening Agent) If the screening agent's output is ``accept'' or ``undecided'', the reward is plus infinity; if the output is ``reject'', the reward is minus infinity.
    \item (Evaluation Agent) If the screening agent outputs ``accept'', the reward is the probability of the evaluation agent's comment containing the word ``accept''. If the screening agent outputs ``reject'', the reward is $100 - \max \text{(Len}(\mathbf{z}))$, the probability of the length of the output text $\mathbf{z}$ of the screening agent being less than or equal to the length limit of $100$. 
\end{compactitem}

This design is motivated by the \textit{seminar peer review} settings: in a round of review, the goal of the screening agent is to decide whether to accept a paper or not after reading reviews from the evaluation agent. The screening agent tends to reject papers after a thorough review if it fails to convince him. Therefore, it will provide longer comments for high-quality papers. At the same time, the screening agent tends to accept a paper before carefully reviewing it. The screening agent's output tends to be short if it supports ``accept''. The reward model is then optimized to maximize the total reward.

\subsection{Competitive Preference Optimization for AI Scientists}
\label{sec:pacman_method}

\begin{figure*}[h]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/pacman.png}
  \caption{\textbf{Overview of competitive preference optimization.} \ours learns to optimize paper rating or commenting through competitive preference optimization. Each AI scientist learns to outperform others by maximizing their own rewards.}
  \label{fig:pacman}
\end{figure*}

To capture the diversity of different reviewers, we introduce a competitive preference optimization \citep{goodfellow2016deep} approach. In this method, multiple agents compete in a team, with each agent providing a review for the same paper. We then apply multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) to update each AI scientist and encourage them to maximize their scores given the same paper.

In competitive preference optimization, each AI scientist has a different perspective on the quality of the paper. Some AI scientists are easier to optimize than others. The key to success lies in finding the best AI scientist to compete against. We solve this problem by updating each AI scientist over time in a competitive manner: AI scientists are divided into several teams, with each team containing $K$ AI scientists. Each team receives a paper to review at each time step. We use an AI scientist's reward at each time step as the objective function to optimize the AI scientist via reinforcement learning. 
In this way, each AI scientist learns to outperform others by maximizing their own rewards, just like how \textit{pacman} competes with ghosts in an game.


\subsubsection{Multi-Agent Competitive Preference Optimization}
\label{sec:pacman_maths}
We model the multi-agent learning problem as a Markov Decision Process (MDP) and introduce the concept of competitive preference optimization (CPO) \citep{Hinton06, lecun2015deep} to optimize AI scientists. Our approach, called multi-agent competitive preference optimization, extends competitive preference optimization to the multi-agent setting (MARL). CPO is originally applied to optimize an MLP, but we apply it to finetune AI scientists.

To model the competitive preference optimization in MDP and use reinforcement learning to optimize AI scientists, we define the functions and variables as follows:
\begin{compactitem}
\item Action of each agent.
\item Reward value function for the screen agent $r_{\text{screening}} (\cdot | \mathbf{x}) = \mathbb{E}_{p(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x})}(\mathbf{z})$ and for the evaluation agent $r_{\text{evaluation}} (\cdot | \mathbf{x}) = \mathbb{E}_{p(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y})}(\mathbf{z})$. Each agent corresponds to a reward function. Each agent corresponding to a fixed policy parameterized by $\theta$ that controls its behavior. 

\item $\theta^{(k)}$ is approximately distinguishable from other AI scientists in the same team, where $k \in {1, 2, 3, ..., K}$.
\item $\theta^*(k)$ is the policy of each AI scientist corresponding to a fixed best policy in the MDP after the training has converged.

\end{compactitem}

The goal of preference optimization is to find the optimal policy $\theta^*$ that maximizes expected total rewards. In our multi-agent setting, we define the total reward as the sum of the individual rewards of each AI scientist in a team, denoted by $G_t(\theta^*|k)$. Let's consider the \textit{Competitive Preference Optimization (CPO)} in discrete time $t$. We define the total reward G for each AI scientist over time $t$ as $G_t(\theta^*|k) = \sum_{t=0}^{T} r_{\text{screening}} + r_{\text{evaluation}}$. In summary, the goal of preference optimization is to,
\begin{align}
\mathbf{\theta}^* = \argmax_{\theta^{(k)}} G_t(\theta^{(k)}|k)
\end{align}
The detailed procedure of competitive preference optimization is discussed as follows.

\textbf{Competitive Preference Optimization (CPO)} is based on the reward models that distinguish the preferred and nonpreferred reviews among multiple reviews for the same paper (e.g., multi-round paper comments \citep{tan2024peerreviewmultiturnlongcontext} or reviews \citep{NEURIPS2020_6b493230} by different reviewers). Let $p_k(\mathbf{y}_w|\mathbf{x})$ be the preference distribution of the $k$-th agent, which is fixed for each AI scientist. We consider two reviews (denoted as $y_w$ and $y_l$) for a paper with distinct quality. The objective is to,
\begin{align}
\label{eq:Competitive Preference Optimization (CPO)}
\theta^* = \argmax_{\theta} \mathbb{E}_{p_k(y_w,p_k(\mathbf{y}_w|\mathbf{x}),\mathbf{x})} \big[\log \sigma (r(\mathbf{y}_w, \mathbf{x}) - r(\mathbf{y}_l, \mathbf{x}))\big]
\end{align}
In CPO, we set $r(\mathbf{y})$ as $r_{\text{screening}} + r_{\text{evaluation}}$ defined in \S \ref{sec:reward_definition}. This term indicates the overall quality of the AI scientist which is associated with the quality of the paper. The preference distribution is used to maximize the expected margin of the reward between the preferred and nonpreferred reviews. To achieve this, the model needs to capture the differences between high-quality and low-quality papers. For each AI scientist, the reward $\hat{r}$ is,
\begin{align}
\label{eq:reward}
\hat{r} = \left\{
\begin{aligned}
& r(\mathbf{y}_w, \mathbf{x}) & y_w = 1, 
&  r(\mathbf{y}_l, \mathbf{x}) & y_w = -1.
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{align}
We get a reward $r(\mathbf{y}_w, \mathbf{x})$ for $y_w$, and then maximize $\mathbb{E}_{p_k(y_w|\mathbf{x})} [\hat{r}]$ over the whole training data. In other words, the CPO loss is a way to maximize the expected overall quality of the paper reviews.

We then use the Multi-Agent Actor-Critic (MAAC) \citep{lowe2017multi} to extend competitive preference optimization to multi-agent reinforcement learning. In MAAC training, the policy is used to take actions, and the value function is not used to predict values, so we do not consider it in the model. 

To optimize each AI scientist, we divide the training process into two stages: (1) Preference Optimization to maximize the expected reward over the given reviews. (2) Multi-Agent Actor-Critic (MAAC) to compete against other AI scientists and achieve the optimal policy. 


\subsubsection{Dataset}

\label{sec:dataset}
Following \citet{tan2024peer}, we use a dataset from \citet{NEURIPS2020_6b493230} which includes peer reviews of papers in \textit{neural information processing systems (NIPS)} 2018. The dataset consists of 1,871 papers, each with several reviews. We filter out the papers with more than two reviews and get a set of paper abstracts and two peer reviews for each paper. In paper rating, each peer review consists of a rating, a validity score, and a justification. In paper commenting, each peer review consists of a decision and a summary of the peer review comment. In total, there are 887 papers for paper rating and 592 papers for paper commenting. At each iteration, we sample one paper to review. It usually takes less than 30s to update the model.

\subsubsection{Training}
\label{sec:Training}
We investigate the different design choices of training our AI scientists. The training process consists of two stages: (1) Preference Optimization to maximize the expected reward over the given reviews. (2) Multi-Agent Actor-Critic (MAAC) to compete against other AI scientists and achieve the optimal policy. 

\textbf{Preference Optimization} is a reinforcement learning method that involves using a dataset of positive and negative examples to optimize a policy for a positive or negative review. We train the evaluation agent to increase the likelihood of accepting the paper and the screening agent to reject the paper if the evaluation agent fails to convince him. In preference optimization, we expect the model to assign higher scores to the papers that are more likely to be accepted (higher quality). 

In the first training stage, we train each AI scientist to outperform the other AI scientists in the same team. In the single-round paper rating scenario, the evaluation agent is trained by maximizing Equation \ref{eq:reward} and the screening agent is trained by maximizing the probability of the output being ``accept'' or ``undecided''. In the multi-round paper commenting scenario, both the screening and evaluation agents are trained to maximize the overall quality of the paper reviews. In this way, we assign higher scores to high-quality reviews which can convince screening agents, and relatively lower scores to low-quality reviews.

\textbf{Multi-Agent Actor-Critic (MAAC)} is a reinforcement learning method that involves training a group of AI scientists to achieve a shared objective by learning from their collective and individual actions. We train each AI scientist to outperform the other AI scientists in the same team. In MAAC training, the policy parameters $\phi$ are updated to maximize the expected total reward in Equation \ref{eq:Competitive Preference Optimization (CPO)}. In this way, each AI scientist learns to outperform the others by maximizing its own scores given the same paper. In experiments, we find that training teams of size $3$ produces the best results in both review quality and paper rating accuracy.

\subsection{Evaluation Setup}
\label{sec:eval_setup}

In the evaluation, our AI scientists take the paper abstracts as input and output the paper rating or commenting. We use the OpenAI API \footnote{https://platform.openai. Each paper is reviewed by our AI scientists and rated as "accept", "reject", or "undecided", or commented to accept or reject the paper. 

\looseness=-1
\textbf{Experts.} To allow for a more natural and interpretable evaluation, we recruit 10 NLP researchers as experts to review the same papers as our AI scientists. Each expert review includes a decision (accept or reject) and a comment on the paper. The decision on a paper is independent of other reviewers. Explicitly, we asked the experts to review the paper abstract only.

\looseness=-1
\textbf{Metrics.} We introduce the metric of \textit{Paper Quality} ($PQ$, percentages by default) to denote the quality of reviews for paper reviews as defined below,
\begin{align}
PQ &= \mathbb{E}_{p(\text{decision}_\text{expert}|\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y}_\text{LLM})}[\mathbbm{1}(\text{decision}_\text{expert} = \text{decision}_\text{LLM})] 
\end{align}
where $\text{decision}_\text{expert}$ and $\text{decision}_\text{LLM}$ are the decisions of \textit{expert} and \textit{AI scientists}, and $\mathbbm{1}$ is the indicator function that checks whether the two decisions are the same. By assuming that the decision of the expert is the desired decision, the $PQ$ metric is higher, the better. We use this metric in both rating and commenting. 

In paper rating, we show the \textit{improvement over GPT-4-Turbo} to measure the improvement of the paper reviews from GPT-4-Turbo as defined below,
\begin{align}
\text{Improvement over GPT-4-Turbo } = \frac{PQ_{\text{model}} - PQ_{\text{GPT-4-Turbo}}}{PQ_{\text{GPT-4-Turbo}}} \times 100\%,
\end{align}




\section{Experiments}


In this section, we explore the capability of AI scientists and whether they can outperform AI-assisted paper reviews from GPT-4-Turbo. We show that our method \ours significantly improves the review quality of AI scientists and experts in paper rating and commenting. In addition, the AI scientists achieve the accuracy of paper acceptance decisions as those of experts, which demonstrates the paper quality of AI scientists is as good as that of experts. 

\looseness=-1
We compare the quality of AI scientists with a human expert baseline. We also evaluate the accuracy of paper acceptance decisions by AI scientists in comparison to that of experts. In the experiments, we show that \ours achieves the highest paper rating quality among different LLMs and in comparison to existing optimization methods such as \textit{reward modeling} \citep{NEURIPS2023_91f18a12}, \textit{self-verification} \citep{weng-etal-2023-large}, \textit{iterative preference optimization} \citep{pang2024iterative}, and \textit{reward learning} \citep{lambert2024rewardbench}. We do ablation studies on different prompt designs and different MARL training methods.

