\subsection{Insights for Scientific Writing} 
\label{sec:ai_scientists_consistencies_insights}

We use the paper rating scenario to make \ours provide \textit{insights} for what characterizes good papers. We use \textit{word frequency} as a measure of general preferences. We find that the most common positive keywords and negative keywords in paper abstracts and reviews are shared by different AI scientists as shown in Figure \ref{fig:case_statsiics}. We use Figure \ref{fig:methods_overview} (b) as the case study to highlight the most common keywords. First, we analyze the frequency of positive keywords and negative keywords in the paper abstracts of rejected papers in Figure \ref{fig:case_statsiics}. The most common positive keywords in paper abstracts are ``\textit{approach}'', ``\textit{method}'', ``\textit{novel}'', and ``\textit{computational}''. The most common negative keywords are ``\textit{conference}'', and ``\textit{previously}''. We further analyze the frequency of positive keywords and negative keywords in screening agent reviews, which reflects the comments on the weak points of rejected papers. The most common positive keywords are ``\textit{benefits}'', ``\textit{approach}'', and ``\textit{advance}''. The most common negative keywords are ``\textit{used}'', ``\textit{previously}'', and \textit{released}''. These keywords may reflect the improvements our AI scientists can offer to improve the overall quality of paper submissions. For example, scientists can avoid releasing their ideas on platforms like the \textit{Cognet AI Paper Workshop} early in their careers. In addition, these keywords provide insights into what reviewers are looking for. In Figure \ref{fig:case_statsiics}, we show the most common keywords of rejected papers in different paper datasets. The NIPS dataset has the most common positive keyword, while the ACL dataset has the most common negative keywords. This may reflect the most common characteristics of papers rejected in different datasets and how reviewers make decisions.
\looseness=-1
In summary, we consider two extreme cases in the optimization: self-reinforcement learning where an AI scientist trains itself and single-agent preference optimization where there is only one AI scientist in one team. In the experiments, we do not observe any benefits from these two optimization methods. In the experiments, the AI scientists do not improve their paper quality in both paper rating and commenting. We think that it comes from two perspectives: (1) In preference optimization, an AI scientist learns to outperform other AI scientists in different teams. There is a huge space to improve the quality of paper reviews. Notably, GPT-4-Turbo achieves low scores in our experiments. Notably, GPT-4-Turbo tends to give low scores, while GPT-3.5-Turbo provides high scores. This difference stems from the optimization method and the number of in-context examples. GPT-4-Turbo uses the reward model in RLHF during training while GPT-3.5-Turbo is not. Notably, GPT-4-Turbo uses more in-context examples than GPT-3.5-Turbo (total: 8 vs. 4). (2) In MAAC training, each AI scientist learns to outperform the others in the same team. We think that it comes from the diversity of AI scientists that makes them focus on different perspectives. In summary, we think that AI scientists and optimization methods in different teams significantly improve the overall paper quality.



\section{Related Works}



\textbf{Review Generation and Review Summarization}. Review generation aims to create a review based on paper details and reviews from other annotators, while review summarization focuses on condensing key information for quick overview.

\textbf{AI Scientists}. AI scientists can provide high-quality comments on scientific papers and offer constructive feedback \citep{liu2024towards, wang2024autosurvey, li2024mlrcopilotautonomousmachinelearning, baek2024researchagent, hu2024automated, yang2024collaborative}. 

\textbf{Reward Modeling and Optimization}. Reward models are used in reinforcement learning to predict the quality of state transitions in AI agents. They can be trained through imitation learning, which optimizes a model to predict human preferences \citep{Bengio+chapter2007, hutter2001towards, lenat1977automated, lenat1983eurisko}. Direct Preference Optimization \citep{rafailov2023direct} and RewardBench \citep{lambert2024rewardbench} are a series of methods that use preference data to train the reward model. These methods are used in LLM fine-tuning \citep{zhu2024safetylock, lee2024rlaifvsrlhfscaling, an2023learning, xiong2024iterativepreferencelearninghuman, yuan2024self}. In comparison, Iterative Preference Optimization \citep{pang2024iterative} assumes that preference data is noisy and uses an iterative approach. In this paper, we introduce a competitive preference optimization approach that applies preference optimization to a multi-agent setting. 

\textbf{Paper Review}. Peer review is crucial in scientific research. New research ideas and ideas that challenge existing paradigms need thorough discussion with other researchers. However, the vast number of research papers makes peer reviewing challenging. It is estimated that about 400,000 paper reviews are needed every year just for CS papers and AI papers, but only about 250,000 researchers are available to provide these reviews \citep{liu2023reviewergpt, hosseini2023fighting, zhang2022investigating}. To address this, researchers have explored ways to improve review efficiency and quality using natural language processing (NLP), including AI-assisted paper reviewing to enhance accuracy and fairness \citep{liang2024can, li2024ai4r}. Recent works have begun experimenting with AI models to automate the review process \citep{liu2023reviewergpt, jin2024agentreviewexploringrejection, tan2024peerreviewmultiturnlongcontext, du2024llms, dyer2024llmagent, du2024llms, wang2024autosurvey, yang2024integrating, tyser2024ai}. 

\textbf{Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning}. Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) involves interacting with an environment through decision-making \citep{lowe2017multi}. Multi-agent systems learn from interactions and experience while trying to maximize rewards. Multi-agent system optimization involves exploring and evolving the state space. We apply multi-agent actor-critic \citep{lowe2017multi} to update AI scientists, involving them in competitive preference optimization. 




\section{Discussion}


We take inspiration from the \textit{seminar paper review} to design a multi-agent learning system where AI scientists compete in making paper reviews. We find that AI scientists achieve paper quality comparable to that of experts and even provide more constructive feedback. The limitations of this work are as follows: (1) Training LLMs requires significant hardware resources, resulting in long training times. It takes 12 hours to train $8\times$80G LLMs. (2) We use third-party LLMs to be AI scientists without fine-tuning them. It would be valuable to use techniques like LoRA \citep{hu2022lora}, ZeRO \citep{rajbhargava2021zeo}, and FastFOQ \citep{kuzmin2024fp8quantizationpowertwoexponent}, and fine-tune them on paper reviews to improve the performance. (3) We only consider two scenarios in paper reviewing. It would be valuable to allow AI scientists to have more interactions in the multi-round paper commenting. (4) We only consider paper reviews where each paper is independently reviewed. It would be valuable to allow agents to have cross-paper discussions like those between experts8} to discuss papers.

In summary, we present \ours, which involves AI scientists competing with each other to improve their performance in making paper reviews. \ours applies a competitive preference optimization approach to update AI scientists in different scenarios: rating-based paper review and comment-based paper review. In experiments, we show that \ours outperforms other models in GPT-4-Turbo, IPO-GPT, and IPO-GPT-3.5-Turbo in paper rating. Moreover, \ours provides better reviews than experts and AI scientists in paper commenting. \ours achieves the accuracy of paper acceptance decisions as those of experts. In addition, \ours provides insights into what characteristics optimize the paper quality from the perspective of AI scientists. We think that it comes from the diversity of AI scientists that makes them focus on different perspectives. In the future, we plan to optimize more scenarios in paper reviewing and allow more AI scientists to have cross-paper discussions to further improve their performance. 

