\section{Preliminaries}

\label{sec:preliminaries}
\subsection{Problem Formulation}

Given a peer review process, given one peer review report, we denote its score for a research paper as $s$ and its corresponding comment as $c$. The score $s$ is defined to be one of \{Excellent, Very Good, Good, Fair, Poor$\}$. The peer review report with its score $s$ is provided by a reviewer who has expertise in a specific field $r$. Our task is to design a multi-agent system to generate as many review reports and scores that are as close to human reviews for research papers as possible.

\subsection{Multi-agent System Design}

We define multi-agent system design~\citep{Hu2024AutomatedDO} as the automated design of a multi-agent system to achieve a given set of task requirements, during the stages of agent creation and deployment. The design of a multi-agent system least involves the automated selection of agents and their organization, such as the field of each agent and the role assignment during task completion.

\subsection{Language Agent}
\label{sec:preliminaries:lang_agent}

Given a language model $\mathcal{M}$ and an input sequence of tokens $x=(x_1,x_2,\cdots,x_t)$, where $x_i$ represents a token (i.e. a word or subword), the model $\mathcal{M}$ estimates the probability distribution (a.k.a the language modeling distribution) over possible organic follow-up tokens:
$
    \mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{M}}(x_{t+1}|x_1,\cdots,x_t) \in \mathbb{S}
$
Without further notice, we sample tokens with the distribution $\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{M}}$ when inference.

Language agents are LLMs trained or prompted to provide the required outputs such as written texts, action plans, or codes \citep{hu2022lora, wang2024loragalowrankadaptationgradient, kuzmin2024fp8quantizationpowexponent}. Our goal is to create an autonomous agent that can complete tasks in the environment autonomously. This has been explored in open-domain research~\citep{hu2024automated} and machine learning~\citep{huang2024mlagentbench,liang2024mapping}. In this paper, we specifically focus on the open-domain task of scientific peer reviewing, which is more challenging than other open-domain tasks such as writing and revising research papers because it requires multiple expertise-bound knowledge.

\subsection{Multi-turn Dialogue}
\label{sec:preliminaries:multi-turn_dialogue}

We define multi-turn dialogue as a series of interactions involving one or more participants with an AI assistant~\citep{wei2022chain}. In a multi-turn dialogue, participants take turns to share information and perform a sub-task or solve a problem. In peer review, we consider a three-turn dialogue involving a gatherer agent, a specialized agent, and the paper to be reviewed. The gatherer agent first provides a summary of the paper. In cases where a specific point needs further examination, the gatherer agent hands over control to a specialized agent, who then provides their analysis after reviewing the paper. Finally, based on all the information, the gatherer prepares an overall recommendation for the paper.


\section{Method}

\label{sec:method}
In this section, we first introduce our multi-agent system design which includes the meta manager and three types of agents (Sec.~\ref{sec:method:multi-agent}). Next, we describe the workflow involving these agents during task completion (Sec.~\ref{sec:method:workflow}).

\subsection{Multi-agent System Design}
\label{sec:method:multi-agent}
In our multi-agent system, a meta-manager agent $\mathcal{M}$ is introduced to automate the design and organization of a multi-agent system. Various language agents $\mathcal{S}_1, \mathcal{S}_2,\cdots,\mathcal{S}_n$ (a.k.a. specialized agents) %
are utilized to provide expertise-bound knowledge required by the task. Additionally, a language agent $\mathcal{G}$ (a.k.a. gatherer agent) is employed to integrate information from other agents and to coordinate various sub-tasks in the system. The example of this multi-agent system using the codebase is illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:overview}.

\noindent\textbf{Meta Manager Agent.}
Once activated, the meta manager agent automatically selects $k$ (typically $k>1$) experts and assigns roles for them. During this process, it utilizes information from other agents to determine their fields and corresponding roles (e.g. reviewer, reviser, etc). It maintains a database containing information about other agents in the multi-agent system such as their fields, capabilities, etc. Once the multi-agent system is deployed, it is able to autonomously analyze the task requirements and then adjust the deployment as needed.

\noindent\textbf{Specialized Agents.}
Various language models fine-tuned or prompted in specific contexts or domains are used as specialized agents $\{\mathcal{S}_1,\mathcal{S}_2,\cdots,\mathcal{S}_n\}$, and also include expert agents with domain-specific expertise. Typically, specialized agents are expert agents that have domain expertise but are unable to generalize their capabilities across multiple domains due to a lack of multi-tasking abilities. Most language models possess expertise or knowledge in a specific domain and can perform open-domain tasks. Consequently, they are well-suited for use as specialized agents in our multi-agent system. Furthermore, we harness the expertise of other language agents that have been specifically trained or optimized for different expertise domains.

\noindent\textbf{Gatherer Agent.}
The gatherer agent $\mathcal{G}$ is a language model trained or prompted to integrate information from different sources into a coherent and organized format. It can perform various tasks, such as analyzing different information, compiling different information sources, summarizing different information sources, etc.
It plays a significant role in facilitating multi-turn dialogue by synthesizing expert feedback and providing optimized suggestions to enhance the efficiency of multi-round reviews of research papers.

\subsection{Workflow}
\label{sec:method:workflow}

Once a peer review request is received, the meta manager agent $\mathcal{M}$ is activated and begins deploying other required agents. Typically, multiple specialized agents $\{\mathcal{S}_i\}$ are activated, which may all be copies of a single language model. Subsequently, a gatherer agent $\mathcal{G}$ activated is deployed and the multi-agent system starts its workflow. As shown in Figure \ref{fig:overview}, %
the specific workflowconducted by the deployed agents can be formulated as follows:

\noindent \textbf{Step 1}: The gatherer agent $\mathcal{G}$ samples its follow-up action with $[\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{M}}(x_{t+1}|x_1,\cdots,x_t)|x_{t} = \sum_{i=1}^{k}{\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{S}_i}(x_{t+1}|x_1,\cdots,x_t)}$, where $k$ specialized agents $\{\mathcal{S}_1,\mathcal{S}_2,\cdots,\mathcal{S}_k\}$ are deployed and possess the ability to follow the dialogue.
\noindent \textbf{Step 2}: The multi-turn dialogue conducted by the gatherer and the specialized agents continues until all information is gathered or the maximum rounds of dialogue is reached. We follow existing works and set the maximum number of rounds of dialogue to be four. This is to avoid excessive context length, which can lead to memory consumption and inaccuracies in language model predictions.
\noindent \textbf{Step 3}: The gatherer Language agent $\mathcal{G}$ analyzes all the acquired information and prepares its follow-up action with $\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{M}}(x_{t+1}|x_1,\cdots,x_t) = \mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}(x_{t+1}|x_1,\cdots,x_t) + \sum_{i=1}^{k}{\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{S}_i}(x_{t+1}|x_1,\cdots,x_t)}$.
