\subsection{Toy Examples}

In this section, we use three toy examples to test the performance of the meta-manager. In our observation, the agent can successfully deploy itself and other experts and output the required answer for the given task requirements. The example also verifies the correctness of the information stored in the database of the meta-manager agent.

\subsection{Baselines}

To the best of our knowledge, there are no research agents specifically designed for a diverse range of scientific tasks like those provided in our benchmark. Therefore, we use some of the latest popular models as the base model and prompt them to be applicable to the task requirements in our benchmark. The following models or agent frameworks function as experts or agents in their respective fields and are used as baselines for comparison:

\noindent\textbf{Writing.}
Fast-Review-GPT \citep{bao2021predicting} is built on \textit{AgentReview} \citep{jin2024agentreviewexploringpeerreview}, and uses a Mistral-based language model for writing suggestions. It has the ability to detect hallucinations using limited paper information only. In our experiments, we use the Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/mistralai/Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2}}  to conduct our experiments on writing.

\noindent\textbf{Reviewing.}
ReviewGPT \citep{liu2023reviewergpt} is a pioneering exploration of the use of language models to review scientific literature. It is built on a GPT-3.5 model and is able to provide suggestions for peer reviewers. Its limited context window restricts its ability to summarize longer papers, so we use GPT-3.5-Turbo to conduct our experiments on reviewing.

\noindent\textbf{Evaluating.}
We use \textit{Chain-of-Thought} Prompting \citep{wei2022chain} on the state-of-the-art 13B language model \textit{GPT-4} to conduct experiments on writing and reviewing. For the evaluation of research papers, we directly use the advanced model of \textit{GPT-4}. We employ the Access AI API\footnote{\url{https://api.accessai. 

\noindent\textbf{AgentFrameworks.}
We use \textit{ReVer \citep{weng-etal-2023-large} as the gatherer agent. OpenAI ChatGPT 3.5 and Mistral 7B are encapsulated into the gatherer agent to act as specialized agents for writing and reviewing respectively.

\subsection{Benchmark}
To support comprehensive evaluation (i.e. writing, reviewing, and evaluating), we build a peer review benchmark based on papers open-sourced by the National Science and Technology Library of China and review data from the corresponding authors as peer reviews. %
We gather files of papers and their reviews, covering six different fields: Computer Science and Symbolic Analysis (CSS), Data Science and Machine Learning (DS/ML), Digital Humanities (DH), Education (E), and two sub-fields of Computer Science (Computer Vision (CV) and Machine Learning (ML)).
A total of 1,469 papers and 3,035 blind review reports covering six fields are included in this dataset. Files are converted into plain text format to standardize the context length during inference.

\subsection{Human Evaluation}
We conduct human evaluation with 35 well-educated participants, including 23 undergraduate students and 12 researchers from the CCF community. They are equally divided into two groups: \textit{Review First} and \textit{Read First}. The two groups receive the same set of content with the order of peer reviews flipped. Participants wear ``blindfolds'' for peer review documents until they submit results to prevent bias. We randomly shuffle the order of the corresponding suggestions to further eliminate the impact of order bias. We conduct experiments in three phases and incorporate the feedback of the previous experiments for improvements.

Participants score or rank outputs produced by the agent based on five dimensions, each rated on a five-point scale. The five dimensions include:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=10pt,itemsep=1pt,parsep=1pt, partopsep=0pt]
\item \textit{Recommended Index (Recom. Index):} This criterion measures the extent to which the agent assists the reviewer in completing the review process. It is evaluated by how easy it is to use the provided information to assign a recommendation.
\item \textit{Full Satisfaction (Full Satis.):} This refers to the degree to which all necessary aspects of the literature research process are covered in the output of the agent.
\item \textit{Feasibility of Implementation (Feas. Implement.):} This criterion measures the agent's ability to assist researchers in completing their tasks.
\item \textit{Satisfaction of Researcher (Satisfact. of Researcher):} This indicates the extent to which the output of the agent satisfies the needs of the reviewer.
\item \textit{User Friendliness of Presentation (User Friendliness):} This criterion measures the clarity of the information provided by the agent. Clear and concise information enhances the efficiency and effectiveness of the review process.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Automated Evaluation}
In addition to human evaluation, we conduct automated evaluation using six models: GPT-4~\citep{achiam2023gpt}, Qwen2.5-14B-chat~\citep{qwen2.5}, Llama3.1-8B~\citep{dubey2024llama3herdmodels}, GPT-3.5~\citep{achiam2023gpt}, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 and Vicuna\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/lmsys/mistral-7b-vicuna-v1.2}}. We use the Access AI API\footnote{\url{https://api.accessai.
We conduct two experiments with different evaluator models: 
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=10pt,itemsep=1pt,parsep=1pt, partopsep=0pt]
\item \textbf{(a) Automated Evaluation} with GPT-4 and Qwen2.5-14B-chat.
\item \textbf{(b) Automated Evaluation} with Llama3.1-8B.
\end{itemize}
In our experiments, we use two different strategies to evaluate the effectiveness of the agents:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=10pt,itemsep=1pt,parsep=1pt, partopsep=0pt]
\item \textbf{(I)} We compare the agent's recommendations with those of human participants and report the percentage of experiments in which the agent is more liked than the human participant. We term it as Achieving Win-rate over Human Expert (A-Win). %
\item \textbf{(II)} We rank the agents based on the same criteria as in human evaluation. We report the average ranking of the agents and also the difference of data distribution between the agent and the baselines.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Result}

\textbf{Human evaluation.} We report the results of human evaluation in Table~\ref{tab:exp_human}, including Win-rate (H-Win) and Agreement (A-Agr). If the agreement score surpasses 50, it indicates that the agent is more satisfied with the output of our system than the human expert.

We find that the gatherer agent using Llama3.1-8B outperforms the baseline agent in writing, and the gatherer agent using GPT-4 outperforms the baseline agent by reviewing. All agents in our system win over the human expert agents in the evaluation of research papers, indicating that the agent system is more satisfied with the output of the system than the human expert.

\textbf{Automated evaluation} with GPT-4 and Qwen2.5-14B-chat is reported in Table \ref{tab:main} (a). It is clear that Llama3.1-8B-based gatherer agent still outperforms other systems. For GPT-4 evaluation, the agent achieves a win-rate of 84.0\% over the human expert, indicating that the output of the agent is of higher quality than that of the human expert. Additionally, the gatherer agent using GPT-4 and Llama3.1-8B also achieves a higher agreement with GPT-4 and Qwen2.5-14B-chat than other agents.

\textbf{Result and Analysis.} 
In all experiments, the gatherer agent outperforms the baseline agent. Additionally, deploying a meta-manager agent can enhance the performance of the gatherer agent and the specialized agent. We analyze the effects of various agents in the multi-agent system. We find that the gatherer and the specialized agents have a positive impact on the output of the system. Also, the meta-manager agent can further enhance the performance of the gatherer and specialized agents based on the information stored in its database and the cooperation between agents within the system.



\section{Autonomous Design of Agentic Systems}



In this section, we introduce a novel framework for building automatic agent systems. We first provide a definition of autonomous agent design and build systems for AI-assisted academic writing and AI-assisted peer reviewing. Secondly, we analyze the quality of the output of the agent system and conduct experiments to verify its effectiveness. Our analysis suggests that multi-agent system design holds significant potential for AI-assisted scientific writing and peer reviewing.

\subsection{Definition}

In this paper, we define \uline{a}uton\textbf{om}ated \textbf{a}gent design as the process of creating a multi-agent system that can operate autonomously. The system is designed so that a meta-agent can effectively manage and deploy multiple specialized AI agents, allowing them to collaborate and achieve their respective tasks without human intervention. This approach leverages the strengths of specialized AI models in diverse fields and aims to minimize human involvement in the design and operation of the system.

The multi-agent system is designed for a task where the input is a peer review request and the output is a peer review report. The system can be generalized to other tasks like research literature analysis or research idea extraction. The system consists of a central manager agent and several specialized AI agents, each with unique expertise in a specific area.
