In Figure~\ref{fig:overview}, we illustrate the conceptual framework of our autonomous system design. In this section, we delve into the details of our multi-agent system designed for AI-assisted academic writing and AI-assisted peer reviewing. It is composed of various language models and AI agents, along with an automated multi-agent design manager agent for automated deployment. We have developed a multi-turn dialogue system that enables communication and information exchange among different agents in the system.

There are three types of models in our autonomous system: the gatherer agent $\mathcal{G}$, the specialized agents $\mathcal{S}_i$, and the meta-manager agent $\mathcal{M}$. We integrate these various language models into a multi-agent system.

The gatherer agent $\mathcal{G}$ is a language model designed to facilitate literature research by providing an overview of a research paper. It can provide detailed comments on the research aspects covered in the paper. It can also summarize, analyze, and prepare feedback based on the paper and previous reviews. 

We utilize a collection of language models as the base model for the specialized agents $\mathcal{S}_1,\cdots,\mathcal{S}_n$. These specialized agents are activated when the gatherer agent or human participant requests assistance with specific questions from experts in their respective domains. Typically, a specialized agent is a language model fine-tuned or prompted in a specific context or domain. In this paper, the specialized agents have two roles: \textit{reviewer} and \textit{evaluator}. Correspondingly, they may possess different capabilities, such as writing, reviewing, and evaluating.

The meta-manager agent $\mathcal{M}$ is a language model optimized to autonomously design and manage a multi-agent system. It dynamically stores the information on other experts such as their fields of expertise, capabilities, and other details during the inference process. The meta-manager agent utilizes its database and computational capabilities to automatically deploy new models as needed.

\subsection{Automatic Human-like Performance}
We analyze the results from agentic systems designed for AI-assisted writing and peer reviewing. We conduct automated evaluation and compare the system's performance with that of human experts. 

In Table~\ref{tab:main} (a), we conduct automated evaluation by using GPT-4 and Qwen2.5-14B-chat as evaluation models. It is evident that the gatherer agent using Llama3.1-8B outperforms other agents. We further conduct automated evaluation using Llama3.1-8B Instruct as the evaluation model, and the results are consistent with those of GPT4. It is evident that, compared to human experts, the agent system demonstrates significant advantages in writing and reviewing tasks, and achieves comparable performance in evaluating research papers. This indicates that the agentic system designed for writing and peer reviewing tasks can achieve human-like performance. We further compare the agreement between GPT-4 and the expert agents in Table~\ref{tab:expert+gpt4}. The agreement between GPT-4 and Gatherer-GPT4 is much higher than that between GPT-4 and Specialized-GPT3.5. 

In summary, our findings demonstrate that the agentic system designed for writing and peer reviewing tasks shows promise in achieving performance similar to that of humans in conducting academic writing and peer reviewing tasks. For writing and reviewing tasks, the agent system even outperforms expert human agents. This highlights the potential of using AI agents to assist scientists in their work.



\section{Conclusion}

In this paper, we propose a novel framework for building automatic agent system. We first introduce a meta-manager agent to organize various language models into a multi-agent system. Then, we construct a dataset that includes 1,469 papers and 3,035 blind review reports covering six different fields for comprehensive agent design and evaluation. We conduct experiments including human evaluation and automated evaluation on our dataset, and show that the agents in our system can effectively cooperate and complete the peer reviewing task, and have been favored by both human and automated evaluations.




