\title{ReviewerNet: Harnessing Multi-Agent Systems for Comprehensive Scientific Paper Review}

\begin{abstract}
Advances in the field of AI-assisted scientific peer review have primarily focused on enhancing individual assistants for writing or revising papers. Given the expertise-heavy and complex nature of peer reviewing, a single agent cannot fully satisfy all needs %
of the peer review process; %
also, multiple agents with different expertise are needed;
existing approaches conduct tests using a limited set of papers and existing agents
lack comprehensive evaluation. In this paper, we propose \ours: a novel approach for building comprehensive %
and comprehensive agentic systems for scientific peer review. In particular, we first design a meta manager for automated design and organization of multi-agent systems, and then integrate various models into a multi-agent system.
To support comprehensive evaluation, we construct a dataset that covers six research fields and includes 1,469 peer-review reports. %
Extensive experiments with both human and automated evaluation demonstrate that our proposed framework is effective in assisting the entire process of scientific peer reviewing. 
We leave the code, data and models in the open source and hope that \ours{} can become a pioneering tool that assists the scientific community with peer reviewing and reading.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}



The role of peer reviewing lies at the heart of the scientific community and is essential to the advancement of scientific progress~\citep{boughton2018research,smith2006peer,oberg2022teaching}. It is the primary mean to ensure that research works are of high quality and addresses issues that others may be unaware of \citep{smith2006peer}. Peer review is regarded as a crucial component in academic conferences and journals to assess and enhance the validity and relevance of scientific research \citep{boughton2018research}. In recent years, large language models (LLMs) have shown promising results in various applications~\citep{achiam2023gpt,reid2024gemini}. They have also been widely used in the generation of scientific texts~\citep{ai4science2023impact,wang2024loragalowrankadaptationgradient}, e.g., writing~\citep{liang2024can} and revising research papers~\citep{hosseini2023fighting}. This has also led to a burgeoning field of using AI to assist or replace humans in the peer review of scientific literature, namely AI-assisted or -empowered scientific peer review~\citep{tyser2024ai}. 
A common approach involves enhancing peer reviewing through pre-processing or post-processing of the content of the paper under review~\citep{du2024llms}. For example, literature research agents such as \textit{AgentReview}~\citep{jin2024agentreviewexploringpeerreview} and \textit{Fast-Review-GPT}~\citep{bao2024fast} focus on evaluating papers in open-domain to provide writing suggestions for researchers, and paper rewriters~\citep{robertson2023gpt4} provide suggestions for revising papers after reviewing. In peer reviewing, many works improve the review quality for papers in machine learning (ML)~\citep{NEURIPS2020_6b493230,zhang2022investigating, su2024analysisicml2023ranking}. \citet{NEURIPS2020_6b493230} explore the use of a ``review agent'' to provide recommendations for revisions for papers submitted to ML conferences such as ICML and ICLR; \citet{su2024analysisicml2023ranking} propose to use authors' opinions as pseudo-reviewers to evaluate papers at ICML 2023, demonstrating the potential of AI-assisted reviewing in ML research (but also pointing out potential risks of such approach).

