Despite advancements in developing literature research agents and scientific paper reviewers, two critical challenges remain. Firstly, the writing and revising of research papers typically require expertise in a variety of specific areas, beyond what a single agent can provide~\citep{dubey2024llama3herdmodels}. For instance, an agent might be adept at offering suggestions for writing experiments but lack the necessary domain knowledge for writing introductory sections. Consequently, to comprehensively assist the production of scientific literature~\citep{wang2023scientific}, it is essential to deploy various agents with diverse expertise. Secondly, evaluation of generated texts for scientific peer reviewing is particularly challenging, as there is no "ground truth" for the generated text, and it is difficult to judge whether the outputs are within the scope of the authors' expertise. Existing benchmarks for scientific peer reviews conduct tests on a limited set of papers, many of which are tailored to a specific research field and crafting preferences using simplified criteria; they cannot represent all types of scientific literature to evaluate the diversity and comprehensiveness of agentic systems.

In this paper, we propose \ours{}: a multi-agent framework for building \underline{review}er\underline{net}work for comprehensive and comprehensive agent design for scientific peer reviewing. Specifically, we first introduce a generic meta-manager agent which aims at the automated design and automated organization of multi-agent systems, and then integrate various language agents into a multi-agent system.
To support comprehensive evaluation, we construct \ours{}: a peer review dataset with peer review reports written by reviewers with expertise in six diverse research fields and include reports on 1,469 research papers. In this way, the agent is organized in a hierarchical structure including three types of models: 1) a literature research agent (\textit{Gatherer}) that focuses on reading papers and generating overviews; 2) experts (specialized agents) in specific fields, that are responsible for a detailed evaluation of the quality of scientific papers; %
3) a meta manager for automatically deploying and replacing agents in the multi-agent system according to the task requirements.

The effectiveness of the multi-agent system of our approach is evaluated by conducting comprehensive experiments in a peer review benchmark. The results show that the agents within the system can effectively cooperate and complete the peer reviewing task, and have been favored by both human and automated evaluations.

In summary, we highlight our core contributions as follows:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=10pt,itemsep=1pt,parsep=1pt, partopsep=0pt]
\item We introduce the \ours{} framework that designs a meta-manager for automated design and organization of multi-agent systems, which integrates various language agents into a multi-agent system.
\item We construct the first peer review dataset that includes six research fields and includes 1,469 peer review reports. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work that builds a multi-agent system for comprehensive and comprehensive agent design.
\item We conduct extensive human evaluation and automated evaluation on our peer review benchmark to show that the multi-agent system in our framework can effectively assist in peer reviewing.
\end{itemize}



\section{Related Work}


\subsection{AI-assisted Scientific Peer Review}
Various works have been conducted on research agents and peer reviewers in recent years. Some research focuses on writing or revising research papers, such as \textit{Fast-Review-GPT} by \citep{bao2024fast} and \textit{AgentReview} by \citet{jin2024agentreviewexploringpeerreview}. Other works target reviewing scientific papers such as \textit{ReviewGPT} \citep{liu2023reviewergpt} and \textit{MLR-Copilot} \citep{li2024mlrcopilotautonomousmachinelearning}. \citet{radensky2024scideator} propose \textit{Scideator}, an agent capable of generating innovative ideas grounded in real-world research. \textit{AutoSurvey} by \citet{wang2024autosurvey} automates literature research and survey generation on scientific papers. \textit{ForPeP} \citep{taniguchi2024collective} introduces a formalized framework for automated scientific peer review and provides an initial baseline. However, these existing works are often limited to a specific field and conduct task-specific evaluations. Consequently, the resulting methods and datasets are tailored to a narrow scope of the field and task, lacking a comprehensive assessment of the techniques developed. Moreover, these works often underestimate the expertise-bound nature of peer reviewing and the fact that multiple experts are required to evaluate scientific papers thoroughly.

\subsection{AI-assisted Scientific Writing}
AI has also been deployed in academic writing in general. The generation of scientific papers as a novel output of AI is a notably promising application and poses significant challenges~\citep{langley2024integrated}. \textit{AI4R} \citep{li2024ai4r} outlines a level of analysis for AI-assisted scientific writing that can facilitate the transformation from data to knowledge. Early works in this direction include \textit{DENDRAL} and \textit{META-DENDRAL} \citep{buchanan1981dendral}, which aim to computer-assist the discovery of organic molecular configurations, even though no molecular structure was found with these tools. 
\citet{langley1987scientific} develops a system to assist scientists in producing scientific claims and papers. \citet{lenat1977automated} \citep{lenat1983eurisko} and \citet{hutter2001towards} propose to use expert heuristics and probabilistic models to automatically discover scientific knowledge. Scimon \citep{wang2023scimon} is trained on tens of thousands of papers in the US patent office and can provide writing inspirations for users. \textit{AgentReview} \citep{jin2024agentreviewexploringpeerreview} and \textit{Fast-Review-GPT} \citep{bao2024fast} are two recent works that focus on writing and revising research papers. They use literature research agents to help scientists by providing writing suggestions or reviewing their paper. 
Researchers in the field of materials science have been pioneering in deploying AI into their domain \citep{pyzer2022accelerating,merchant2023scaling}. \textit{AI4Science} \citep{ai4science2023impact} has created \textit{AI Assistant} \citep{reid2024gemini}, which is designed to be helpful in speeding up the scientific discovery and innovation process. The \textit{Dragon} system \citep{liu2024towards} which is an autonomous agent for simulations in materials science, and can be extended to other scientific domains. Similar works have also been done on other domains, e.g. open-domain research~\citep{huang2024mlagentbench}, mathematical discovery~\citep{romera2024mathematical}, biology~\citep{hayes2024simulating} and materials science~\citep{merchant2023scaling}.


\subsection{AI-assisted Sciene and Mathematics}
The emergence of \textit{Deep Learning}~\citep{Hinton06,Bengio+chapter2007,goodfellow2016deep,lecun2015deep} has driven growth of research into the use of AI in digital science and mathematics. There is a growing body of work on AI applications in science and mathematics. For example,
\citet{jumper2021highly} propose a machine learning method \textit{AlphaFold} that predicts the three-dimensional structures of protein molecules from their genetic sequences, and demonstrates the success of AI in predicting protein structure.
\citet{hayes2024simulating} explore the use of language models to simulate the process of natural selection in biology.
Other works in this domain include the use of Monte Carlo simulation~\citep{nuijten2016prevalence}, reinforcement learning~\citep{shinn2023reflexion}, reinforcement learning from AI feedback (RLAIF)~\citep{lee2024rlaifvsrlhfscaling}, and use of probabilistic modeling~\citep{hutter2001towards} in the search for new mathematical expressions and configurations. Other examples include the use of AlphaTensor \citep{clark} for the discovery of new mathematical expressions, and the use of MCMC and MCAR methods for the generation of novel mathematical expressions and structures. 
\subsection{Reward Modelling}
Language models trained for the task of next-token prediction can be fine-tuned with reinforcement learning with human or AI feedback~\citep{cho2024rlaif}. Works like \textit{Reflexion} \citep{shinn2023reflexion}, \textit{VicunaReward} \citep{yuan2024self} and \textit{RewardBench} \citep{lambert2024rewardbench} explore the use of AI feedback in the training of language models in the context of AI-assisted decision-making. \textit{Reward AI} \citep{zhang2024generative} propose to use a reward model trained with AI feedback to provide verifier scores for existing language models. Iterative Preference Optimization (PO) has also been used in the training of language models, and has achieved great success \citep{rafailov2023direct,liu2024iterativelengthregularizeddirectpreference,pang2024iterative}. In this work, we also conduct experiments with iterations of AI feedback during performance testing.

