\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\textbf{Datasets.} We construct a test set with 50 papers accepted by ICLR 2024 and 2025 for experiments. It ensures that \raagen's reviews and feedback have consistent outcomes with that of human reviewers. 

\textbf{Evaluation.} We conduct experiments and quantitative analysis on \textbf{\textit{Higher Accuracy, Richer Information, and More Positive Feedback}}, \textbf{\textit{Significant Improvement in Efficiency}}, \textbf{\textit{Reduction of Reviewer Fatigue}},  \textbf{\textit{Improvement of Feedback Quality with Iterative Reasoning}} and \textbf{\textit{Steered Preference Optimization}}.
Each experiment is conducted on the test set. For each paper, there are 3 reviewers and all 4 reviewer agents involved in the peer review process.

In the experiments, we let each participant in the experiments (including reviewers and \raagen) give their preference votes after comparing the corresponding two pairs including papers, reviews, and feedback. Each paper has one pair of feedback to be compared. For each pair of papers, there are four pairs of feedback to be compared. Each pair of papers have two votes and thus the maximum and minimum scores are 100\% and 50\%.

\textbf{Language Models Used.} %\textit{ChatGLM-3-6b}
\raagen is very flexible and can use various language models to implement different roles. For the experiments, we use \textbf{\textit{Mistral-7b-Instruct-v0.2}} \citep{jiang2023mistral}, \textbf{\textit{Llama3-8b-Instruct}} \citep{dubey2024llama3herdmodels} and \textbf{\textit{Qwen2.5-Chat}} \citep{qwen2.5} as the backbone for language models. 


