\title{Reviewer Agent: Enhancing Scientific Peer Review Experience with Reviewer Agents}

\begin{abstract}
Peer review is in a transition period. It is time-consuming and lacks experticity in reviewers. To address these issues, we build Reviewer Agent as a closed-loop system where all daigons can be controlled and they can interact with each other. Built on this system, we propose a novel scientific peer review system with all the participants, i.e., reviewers, reviewers agents, and reviewers assistant agents. It enables efficient and accurate peer reviewing with almost no time and effort cost. In this way, our system offers three benefits: 1) high-quality reviews and efficient peer reviewing; 2) it eases the workload and waiting time for reviewers; and 3) it enhances and amplifies the role of reviewers in the peer review process.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}




\begin{figure}[h] % 这里是插入图片的代码
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{figures/difficulty_charts.pdf}
    \caption{Percentage of the difficulty level of the papers submitted to ICLR 2024 Peer Review Chair (PRC) Challenge.}
    \label{fig:cl_charts}
\end{figure}
Scientific peer reviewing aims to maintain a high standard of scientific quality for presenting findings. As one of the most democratic and rigorous field, AI research has been deeply influenced by peer reviewing~\citep{langley1987scientific,smith2006peer}. In the era of the large language model (\llm) \citep{achiam2023gpt, reid2024gemini}, large language models can further enhance and improve the peer reviewing \citep{liang2024mapping,wang2024loragalowrankadaptationgradient}. 
Recently, many researchers have explored the potential of \llms to assist in peer reviewing across different scientific fields including but not limited to computer science~\citep{liu2023reviewergpt,liang2024can}, chemistry \citep{hosseini2023fighting} and others \citep{ai4science2023impact}. In the fields of natural language processing (NLP) and relevant artificial intelligence (AI) fields, peer reviewing is becoming increasingly more challenging. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:cl_charts}, more than 60\% of the papers submitted to the ICLR 2024 Peer Review Chair (PRC) Challenge are classified as novel technical topics. This phenomenon is not isolated since many researchers have observed similar situations in their fields and expressed concerns \citep{boughton2018research}. 






Being fundamental to computational science, large language models have the unique ability of extracting intricate patterns within massive amounts of data~\citep{Bengio+chapter2007,Hinton06,lecun2015deep}. This makes \llms adaptable to various tasks that require complex reasoning~\citep{NEURIPS2020_6b493230,pang2024iterative,huang2024mlagentbench}, such as scientific discovery \citep{romera2024mathematical,langley2024integrated}, chemical compound discovery~\citep{merchant2023scaling,pyzer2022accelerating}, biological science \citep{hayes2024simulating,jumper2021highly} and chemical engineering \citep{buchanan1981dendral}. 
To the best of our knowledge, no large language model has been capable of true and autonomous scientific discovery. However, this can change the way that scientific discovery is communicated. Based on that, conducting peer reviewing as but one step in the scientific discovery process also relies on \llms.
In this paper, we propose a novel and efficient peer reviewing system with a closed-loop reviewers agent (\raagen). It relies on a series of daigons, in which the participants can be controlled centrally and thus be interactive with each other based on specific mechanism.
In each daigons, a single reviewer is engaged as one loop element. Instead of directly engaging human reviewers in the peer review of specific papers, human reviewers are relieved from the straggling writing process with time-consuming. In each daigon, \raagen assists the reviewer by emulating the reviewer's role and conducts the review and corresponding related tasks as a reviewers agent. %based on an assistant prompt along with the paper to be reviewed.
On the basis of the review by the reviewers agents, human reviewers are able to efficiently carry out their review work, including summarizing the paper, generating questions, formulating questions, and providing feedback. % and  provide an overall score for the paper
To further assist and enhance the efficiency and accuracy of human reviewers, we design a reviewers assistant agent that can provide high-quality and personalized feedback for each paper. The assistant prompt along with each paper is used to generate the corresponding reviewers assistant agent, which then iteratively refines and self-corrects the corresponding feedback.
Finally, the human reviewers glean valuable insights from multiple rounds of reviews and feedback, ensuring for their quality of peer reviewing. 

The main contributions of this paper are as follows:

\textbf{\textit{A closed-loop system for peer reviewing.}} We propose a closed-loop system for peer reviewing, which is a overloop system including multiple participants, including a human reviewer, a reviewers agent and a reviewers assistant agent. Within the system, all daigons can be controlled centrally, and they can be interactive with each other with well-defined mechanisms.% including papers to be reviewed, reviewers agents, and reviewers assistant agents.

\textbf{\textit{An efficient and expert-level peer reviewing process.}} Built on the closed-loop system for peer reviewing, a highly efficient and expert-level peer reviewing process with multiple round iterations is designed. It allows for iterative interaction between participants, facilitating the provision of expert-level feedback for papers.

\textbf{\textit{A novel design for reviewers assistant.}} We design a reviewers assistant agent that can provide high-quality and personalized feedback for each paper. The assistant prompt along with each paper is used to generate the corresponding reviewers assistant agent, which then iteratively refines and self-corrects the corresponding feedback.

To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to explore \raagen for peer reviewing. Extensive experiments show that: (1) \raagen can significantly enhance the efficiency and accuracy of peer reviewing; (2) it alleviates the pressures on reviewers and improves their experience; (3) the efficiency and accuracy of peer reviewing are further enhanced by reviewers assistant agents. 
