\section{Related Work}

\textbf{Human-AI Interaction.} \raagen leverages large language models to assist human reviewers in the peer reviewing process, facilitating several contemporary works~\citep{liu2023reviewergpt,tyser2024ai,liang2024can,robertson2023gpt4}. \citet{jin2024agentreviewexploringpeerreview} and \citet{tan2024peerreviewmultiturnlongcontext} utilize \llms in a multi-turn and long-context dialogues to enhance the efficiency and improve the way of conducting peer reviews. \citet{zhang2024generative} and \citet{du2024llms} exploit the latent space of language models to train better language models for validation or verifiers to assist the peer reviewing process.
The effects of \llms on the outcomes of peer reviews have been mentioned in many studies \citep{hosseini2023fighting}. \citet{shinn2023reflexion} introduces a novel framework that utilizes feedback from the environment to enhance \llms, thereby increasing the success rate of reviews. The authors also highlight the importance of validation, verification, and correction procedures in the context of \llms employment \citep{achiam2023gpt}. %The application of \llms in peer reviewing has the potential to not only enrich intellectual diversity in academic discourse \citep{hosseini2023fighting}, mitigating the risks of \textit{bias} and \textit{specificity}, but also enable the incorporation and integration of real-time data and feedback \citep{zhang2022investigating,liu2023reviewergpt}.

\textbf{\llms-based Agents.} \raagen relies on large language models to assist human reviewers in the peer reviewing process.
In this paper, \raagen follows the line of works about autonomous agents, including ReAct-S, Reflexion, CARA, Automatic-DAO and so on \citep{hu2024automated,weng-etal-2023-large,shinn2023reflexion,lu2024ai}.  %More complex strategies \citep{Hu2024AutomatedDO} have also been developed which employ reinforcement learning to maximize the number of citations and enhance the readability, novelty, and influence of research articles. 
Specifically, ReAct \citep{hu2024automated} prompts large language models with thought, actions, and observations to solve new tasks.
Reflexion \citep{shinn2023reflexion} trains agents using reinforcement learning and then introduces these agents to help large language models verify their outputs.
CARA \citep{weng-etal-2023-large} proposes a verification process to enhance the reasoning capabilities of \llms.
Automatic-DAO \citep{lu2024ai} leverages a variety of large language models to autonomously complete open-ended scientific exploration tasks.
MOSS \citep{zhu2024mossenablingcodedrivenevolution} is designed to enable efficient and scalable AI agent collaboration in code-driven environments.
Notably, these approaches commonly employ the chain-of-thought prompt, \citet{wei2022chain}, as a fundamental technique to stimulate reasoning and problem-solving abilities of language models.
In this paper, we design a novel and efficient peer review system with a closed-loop system for reviewers along with several daigons including reviewers agents and reviewers assistant agents.

\textbf{Paper Quality Validation.} In peer reviewing, various models have been developed to validate the quality of papers. The validation of papers usually %evaluate paper quality by analyzing and\citep{bao2021predicting} and \citep{wang2023scientific} mainly focus on\citep{nuijten2016prevalence} and \citep{huang2024mlagentbench} combine validation and quality improvement.
This paper focuses on the latter one. A substantial body of research has concentrated on training language models to improve the quality of peer reviews. \citet{kuzmin2024fp8quantizationpowertheexponent} applies Fine-tuning (FT) algorithms to training language models, LoRA \citep{hu2022lora} and GA-LoRA \citep{wang2024loragalowrankadaptationgradient} for efficient parameter updates. The quality of peer reviews is also enhanced by utilizing reinforcement learning techniques, including PPO \citep{10.1145/3394486.3406703}, RLAIF \citep{lee2024rlaifvsrlhfscaling,an2023learning,yang2024collaborative,rafailov2023direct} and RLHF \citep{yuan2024self}.
\citet{liu2024iterativelengthregularizeddirectpreference} have achieved success in enhancing the reasoning and reasoning abilities of language models through Iterative Length-Regularized Direct Preference Optimization (ILDPO).
\citet{xiong2024iterativepreferencelearninghuman} and \citet{liu2024iterativelengthregularizeddirectpreference} demonstrate that iterative preference optimization can enhance the reasoning and reasoning abilities of language models based on human feedback.
In this paper, we further investigate the impact of iterative preference optimization and self-correction mechanisms on the effectiveness of reviewers agents and reviewers assistant agents.

\textbf{Data Augmented Generation (DAG).} DAG is a method used to generate data and enhance the capabilities of language models by leveraging their natural language processing and artificial intelligence skills \citep{collins2017supervised}. DAG involves initially sampling the original text to obtain a partial sequence, which then teaches the language model to predict or generate a complete or contextually related text based on this partial sequence. This partial sequence serves as a mined pattern and seed. The language model is then trained to predict or generate a complete or contextually relevant text based on this partial sequence. In this way, DAG facilitates various language tasks, such as machine translation and text summarization. In this paper, we further explore the potential of DAG in enhancing the efficiency and accuracy of peer reviews by establishing a closed-loop system for peer reviewing. %This closed-loop system, which is not a traditional DAG system, enables us to investigate the potential of DAG in establishing a closed-loop system for peer reviewing.

\textbf{Pear Review Generation.} 
This paper assumes that the papers are automatically checked against problems such as text similarity, modifications of authors, and text categories in advance. The peer review is generated by the reviewers agent or reviewers assistant agent in the system. There are some related works such as GPT00 \citep{hu2022syntheticgpt0}, ChatGPT outperform humans \citep{liu2023reviewergpt}, GPT002 \citep{zhang2024generative}, GPT4 ICLR 2024 Peer Review Chair (PRC)  \citep{NEURIPS2024_24d93986} and ReviewerGPT \citep{liu2023reviewergpt} in the field of peer reviewing.
The peer reviews generated by our system are of higher quality and are addressing specific issues, including consistency, completeness, and accuracy, when compared to these approaches.
Additionally, the reviewers agent and the reviewers assistant agent have self-correction capabilities for real-time updates and corrections of feedback.




\section{Reviewer Agent}


In this section, we first define the symbols and then introduce the setup of \raagen.

\subsection{Setup}

The input of \raagen is denoted as a $D_0$ including a paper to be reviewed $P_0$, a peer review process prompt $\mathcal{S}$, and a language model $\mathcal{M}$. The output is the whole daigon including reviews, feedback, and a overall score. In each round of the daigon, the language model $\mathcal{M}$ produces:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A summary of the paper. In each daigon, we may have multiple daigons for a paper and thus have multiple summaries.
    \item Multi-choice and chain-of-thought reasoning.
    \item Questions, answers and follow-ups.
    \item Feedback on the questions and answers.
    \item Peers view to be further refined.
\end{itemize}

Then, the human reviewer is receiving the output and %to provide an overall score and 
engages in the peer review process including summarizing the paper, generating questions, forming judgments, and providing feedback.
\subsection{A Closed-loop System: Reviewers Agents}

\begin{figure*}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{figures/daigons.pdf}
    \caption{\small{
Multi-round Interaction between human reviewers and language models including reviewers agents and reviewers assistant agents.
    } }
    \label{fig:daigons}
\end{figure*}
A reviewers agent is designed to assist human reviewers in the peer review process as a daigon. Compared to the vanilla language model, the reviewers agent has the following features: (1) it can interact with human reviewers in a multi-round daigon; (2) it can be triggered by human reviewers in the peer review process; and (3) by leveraging \llms, it potentially has a high level of expertise. In this paper, we assume that reviewers agents are provided by expertise for the papers. % this function of reviewers agents in the daigon is very important in the process of reviewing.


Let $D_0$ be the input including a paper to be reviewed $P_0$ and a language model $\mathcal{M}$. 
\begin{itemize}
    \item For the human reviewer, we let $D_0=\emptyset$ and for the human reviewer use each $D_i$, we have $\mathcal{M}(D_i)\rightarrow P_0^i$, which is the summary of the paper. It's important to mention that different daigons may have different summaries.

    \item For the reviewers agent, we let $\mathcal{S}$ be the prompt for the peer review process and we have $D_0=\{\mathcal{S},P_0\}$.

    \item For the reviewers agent in the second round, we have $D_1=\{\mathcal{S},P_0, R_0, R_0^\textit{feedback}, R_0^\textit{decision}\}$ and similarly, for the reviewers agents in the $i$-th round, $D_i=\{\mathcal{S},P_0,R_0,...,R_{i-1},R_{i-1}^\textit{feedback},R_{i-1}^\textit{decision}\}$.


\end{itemize}
where $i\in\{1,2,3,4,4\}$, $R_0=(Q_0,A_0)$ and $Q_0=(q_0, a_0)$ with $q_0$ being the questions and $a_0$ being the answers corresponding to $q_0$. We have $R_0^\textit{feedback}=\mathcal{M}(D_i=\{\mathcal{S},P_0, R_{i-1},R_{i-1}^\textit{feedback},R_{i-1}^\textit{decision}\})\rightarrow R_0$. %It is produced by the reviewers agent in the $i$-th round.

\textbf{The first round daigon for reviewers agents.} The reviewers agent in the first round produces questions and answers along with a peer review based on the paper to be reviewed and the prompt. 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    R_0&=\mathcal{M}(D_0)\rightarrow (Q_0,A_0)\\
    Q_i&=\mathcal{M}(D_0)\rightarrow q_0\\
    A_i&=\mathcal{M}(D_0)\rightarrow a_0\\
    F_i&=\mathcal{M}(D_0)\rightarrow f_0
\end{aligned}
\label{eql:round1}
\end{equation}

Then, the human reviewer is engaged in the peer review process including answering the questions, judging the questions and answers, and providing feedback and further requests for the reviewers agent in the peer review process. 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    R_0^\textit{feedback}&=\mathcal{M}(D_i=\{\mathcal{S},P_0, R_0, R_0^\textit{feedback}\})\\
    &\!\rightarrow\!(R_0^\textit{answer},R_0^\textit{question},R_0^\textit{request},R_0^\textit{score})\!
\end{aligned}
\label{eql:human1}
\end{equation}
$R_0^\textit{answer}$ includes the answers to the questions, and $R_0^\textit{question},R_0^\textit{request}$ include further questions and requests for more details. $R_0^\textit{score}$ represents the overall score for the paper provided by the reviewers agent. %$R_0^\textit{score}$ represents the overall score for the paper from reviewers agents.

\textbf{The following round daigons for reviewers agents.} As the following rounds of daigons, we have the same work as in the first round for the reviewers agent. Equations \ref{eql:round1} can be applied to each reviewers agent for each daigon. %$\{R_i, Q_i, A_i, F_i,i=1,2,...,n\}$.
The human reviewer is then engaged in the peer review process with multiple rounds of daigons. Equations \ref{eql:human1} can be applied to each  reviewers agent for each daigon. 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    R_i^\textit{feedback}&=\mathcal{M}(D_i\!\=\!\{\mathcal{S}\!,P_0, R_0,...,R_{i-1},R_{i-1}^\textit{feedback},R_{i-1}^\textit{decision}\})\\
    &\!\rightarrow\!(R_i^\textit{answer},R_i^\textit{question},R_i^\textit{request},R_i^\textit{score})\!
\end{aligned}
\label{eql:human2}
\end{equation}

In each daigon, the corresponding reviewers agent generates a peer review after interacting with human reviewers including carefully analyzing the paper and providing expert-level, detailed, and accurate feedback.
\subsection{A Data Augmented Generation Mechanism: Reviewers Assistant Agents}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.65\linewidth]{figures/assistants.pdf}
    \caption{
The interaction among a reviewer agent, a human reviewer, and a reviewer assistant agent.
    }
    \label{fig:assistants}
\end{figure}
A reviewers assistant agent is designed to assist human reviewers in the paper review process as a daigon. The function of the assistant agent is to provide high-quality and personalized feedback for each paper. In each daigon, the reviewers assistant agent receives the reasoning and feedback from the reviewers agents produced in the corresponding round and then, it interacts with the human reviewer to provide valuable and personalized feedback and thus, to enhance the quality of the human review.
Formally, the assistant agent is $R_i^\textit{assist}=\mathcal{M}(D_i= \{\mathcal{S},P_0, R_0,...,R_{i}\})\rightarrow R_i^\textit{answer_asp}$, which can be used together with $R_i^\textit{answer}$ to assist the human reviewer to provide better feedback in the peer review process. 
By deploying the reviewers assistant agent into the daigon, we augment the data for conducting peer reviews along with the corresponding reasoning and feedback from the reviewers agents.
Figure~\ref{fig:assistants} illustrates the interaction among a reviewer assistant agent, a human reviewer, and a reviewer agent in a daigon.

To further enhance and refine the feedback and responses provided by the reviewers assistant agent, we propose a self-correction mechanism including iterative reasoning and reward optimization.% such as grammar, calculation, and reasoning errors. 
The iterative reasoning allows for multiple round interactions between human reviewers and the reviewers assistant agent.
The reward optimization is to optimize the feedback generated by the reviewers assistant agent to improve its quality and effectiveness.




\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\textbf{Datasets.} We construct a test set with 50 papers accepted by ICLR 2024 and 2025 for experiments. It ensures that \raagen's reviews and feedback have consistent outcomes with that of human reviewers. 

\textbf{Evaluation.} We conduct experiments and quantitative analysis on \textbf{\textit{Higher Accuracy, Richer Information, and More Positive Feedback}}, \textbf{\textit{Significant Improvement in Efficiency}}, \textbf{\textit{Reduction of Reviewer Fatigue}},  \textbf{\textit{Improvement of Feedback Quality with Iterative Reasoning}} and \textbf{\textit{Steered Preference Optimization}}.
Each experiment is conducted on the test set. For each paper, there are 3 reviewers and all 4 reviewer agents involved in the peer review process.

In the experiments, we let each participant in the experiments (including reviewers and \raagen) give their preference votes after comparing the corresponding two pairs including papers, reviews, and feedback. Each paper has one pair of feedback to be compared. For each pair of papers, there are four pairs of feedback to be compared. Each pair of papers have two votes and thus the maximum and minimum scores are 100\% and 50\%.

\textbf{Language Models Used.} %\textit{ChatGLM-3-6b}
\raagen is very flexible and can use various language models to implement different roles. For the experiments, we use \textbf{\textit{Mistral-7b-Instruct-v0.2}} \citep{jiang2023mistral}, \textbf{\textit{Llama3-8b-Instruct}} \citep{dubey2024llama3herdmodels} and \textbf{\textit{Qwen2.5-Chat}} \citep{qwen2.5} as the backbone for language models. 


