\section{Mechanism Discussion}


In this section, we discuss the mechanisms that make \raagen and its components work.

\textbf{Chain-of-thought Prompt.} 
The reviewers agent produces chain-of-thought reasoning for peer reviewing before generating responses for questions, answers, and follow-up requests, which is consistent with conclusions from \citet{wei2022chain,weng-etal-2023-large}. 
In the daigons, the reviewers agent uses chain-of-thought reasoning to generate high-quality outputs including summaries, questions, answers, follow-up requests, and reward scores for peer reviews.
The reviewers agent uses the corresponding chain-of-thought reasoning to mimic the thought process of an expert reviewer. 
This allows the reviewers agent to deeply comprehend the content of the paper, generate insightful questions, provide detailed answers, and offer valuable feedback with detailed suggestions for improvement. 
As suggested by \citet{oberg2022teaching}, this thorough thought process allows the reviewers agent to go beyond the basic level, thereby providing more helpful comments.
It is important to mention that the reviewers agent can provide expert-level feedback and thereby freeing the human reviewers from unnecessary strain.

\textbf{Prompt Engineering.}
The reviewers agent uses carefully designed prompt engineering to generate high-quality content and responses.
The reviewers agent uses an expert-level prompt that is tailored to the paper to be reviewed in the peer review process.
It allows the reviewers agent to mimic the thought process of an expert reviewer when reviewing the paper.
The reviewers agent also uses carefully designed prompts to generate high-quality content and responses.
This includes one prompt for the general peer review process along with high-level instructions for generating the corresponding content in each round of interaction.
The reviewers assistant agent uses a personalized prompt that summarizes the paper and the key information along with the feedback in the last round of reviews.
This allows the reviewers assistant agent to generate high-quality, personalized feedback based on the key information and feedback in the last round of reviews.

\textbf{Self-correction Mechanism} is used to iteratively refine and enhance the outputs of the reviewers agent and reviewer assistant agent.
The reviewers agent uses multi-party chain-of-thought reasoning to generate the corresponding output.
The self-correction mechanism allows the reviewers agent to find and correct issues in the previous output. 
It can be used to address problems such as grammar, calculation, and reasoning errors.
The reviewer assistant agent uses the real-time updates from human reviewers and the corresponding feedback in the last round to generate high-quality, personalized feedback for the human reviewers.
This allows the reviewers assistant agent to refine and enhance the quality of the feedback based on the updates from the human reviewers and the corresponding feedback in the last round.




\section{Limitations \& Future Works}


The reasoning ability and the ability to validate ideas or conclusions of \raagen can be improved. The system may also have some limitations as described in the Introduction section, which needs to be further addressed in future works.
It is possible to create biased or discriminatory reviews if the system is trained on biased data.
The data used in the system could also be vulnerable to attack or leakage.
The system could be misused for generating or disseminating false content.
The laws and regulations in some jurisdictions may require human oversight in every stage of the peer review process.
In the future, we shall further explore and expand on the following aspects to improve the performance of \raagen.
It is possible to use a more advanced reinforcement learning method in the daigons to train agents to provide agents more information and thus improve the quality of the reviews.
It is possible to use a multi-agent system with autonomous agents including reviewers agents and human reviewers \citep{lowe2017multi} in the daigons to provide more comprehensive and objective feedback on the paper.
It is also possible to integrate more advanced agents or tools, such as a detailed review generator or an idea refinement tool, to enhance and improve the system \citep{wang2023scimon}. 


\section{Conclusion}

