Deep learning has been applied to various scientific tasks \citep{jumper2021highly,hayes2024simulating,ai4science2023impact, merchant2023scaling,pyzer2022accelerating}. Among them, artificial intelligence (AI) assisted scientific discovery based on large language models (LLMs) has received much attention in recent years \citep{langley1987scientific,langley2024integrated}. \citet{wang2023scientific} argue that the process of scientific discovery can be autonomously accelerated and steered by AI. They propose a Scientific Discovery Engine (SDE), which uses LLMs to streamline the end-to-end scientific workflow from experimental design to obtaining results. \citet{lu2024ai} further introduce an LLM-powered autonomous agent to replace the complex, team-oriented process of scientific discovery with a simpler, faster one. These works are revolutionary yet unsupported by experimental evidence, as they do not demonstrate the capability of LLMs to replace humans in every process of science. 

In fact, peer review, which aims to evaluate the validity and advance the novelty of discoveries, is a critical part of the process of scientific discovery \citep{boughton2018research}. It remains critical in an automated and an idealized scenario because it not only provides quality assurance for the soundness of findings \citep{smith2006peer}, but also provides a basis for iterative development of materials in experimental design \citep{bengio2015scaling}. Therefore, we argue that a peer review system must be automated before the entire process of scientific discovery can be fully automated.

Recently, works have explored the automation of peer reviewing using LLMs. For example, ReviewGPT \citep{liu2023reviewergpt} represents a pilot exploration. However, its design has limitations, as it does not explicitly promote LLMs' attention to a key word like ``evidence'', and the output is sometimes unsatisfactory. ReviewBot \citep{jin2024agentreivewexploringpeerreview} is another approach introduced by \citet{jin2024agentreivew} to simulate peer review through a dialogue framework. It introduces a formalized context (paper structure, etc.) in the prompt.
But such formalization overlooks the fact that an actual human writer often modifies the structure of the paper to add evidence during the revision process. In contrast, it enforces the model to strictly adhere to the original structure. As a result, ReviewBot's performance is not ideal. MAVer \citep{darcar2024maver} introduces a reference-based approach to compare the quality of model reviews with those of humans. The study indicates that MAVer's performance is more robust and better than that of ReviewGPT \citep{liu2023reviewergpt}. Nevertheless, MAVer still lacks robustness in terms of completeness and emergence, and is lacking in comparison with human reviews.

In this study, we introduce \ours, a framework for writing-focused peer review that utilizes language agents. \ours employs a language agent to comprehend the writing styles in target scientific domains and concentrates on key concepts and concepts (e.g., ``evidence''). The quality-focused fine-tuning process enhances LLMs' comprehension of scientific domains and results in significant improvements in the quality of model-generated reviews. Additionally, \ours includes a multi-round review process for iterative improvements of writing feedback. This process involves language agents offering feedback to writer-LLMs based on generated writing outputs. The writing feedback then aims to improve specific writing and is given to the writer-LLM for revision. Through this process, the proposed \ours can achieve a 91\% winrate against human reviews in related papers.

The primary contributions of this paper are as follows: First, we introduce the \ours framework, which automates peer reviews by providing fine-tuning and automated LLM feedback based on writing outputs. Furthermore, we propose an iterative reasoning and preference optimization method to further enhance the quality of writing feedback and reviews. Second, we validate the effectiveness of \ours with comprehensive experiments across various domains and models, achieving a 91\% winrate against human reviews in related papers.


\section{The WUPReview Framework}


\subsection{Overview}
Given the paper to be reviewed, the author and their writing background, and domain-specific requirements, the goal of \ours is to write a high-quality, tailored review that critically evaluates the paper. To achieve this, \ours contains two stages: 1) Quality-focused fine-tuning, which aims to enhance the quality of model-generated reviews, and 2) Multi-round review with iterative optimization.

\Cref{fig:overview} illustrates the process flow of \ours. Step 1 of the fine-tuning stage is to tailor the LLM to the writing requirements using LoRA \citep{hu2022lora} (Low-Rank Adaptation). The optimization is then based on chain-of-thought (CoT) \citep{wei2022chain} writing outputs, using the `` évidence'' (evidence) keyword as a proxy to assess reasoning quality. Note that the evidence keyword itself is not the main focus of this paper, and other keywords can be applied. Step 2 is to provide automated refinement and iterative learning based on writing feedback. Specifically, we design prompts to control language agents, enabling them to provide feedback based on the writing outputs. Then, the writing feedback aims to improve specific writing, which is further given to the writer-LLM for revision.

In this process, the model with LoRA (the one optimized by the first step) acts as the writer-LLM, which generates the initial writing. The original model then serves as a reviewer-LLM, which generates a higher-quality writing based on the feedback from the language agent. This step is performed iteratively until convergence. The detailed prompt design is shown in \Cref{app:prompt}. The process is summarized as Algorithm \ref{alg:wupreview}:
\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\small
    \caption{\ours Framework}
    \label{alg:wupreview}
    \setlength{\itemsep}{1pt}
    \SetKwFunction{background}{background}
    \SetKwFunction{document}{document}
    \SetKwFunction{evidence}{evidence}
    \begin{algorithmic}[1]
        \Require Paper to review \emph{\emph{$paper$}}, writer \emph{\emph{name}}, and domain 
        \emph{$domain$}
        \Ensure \emph{\emph{$review$}}
        \State {\textbf{reasons, writing$_{\emph{r}} \gets$ \background($paper, domain$)} 
        \Comment{Get the \emph{scientific background} from GPT-4 \citep{achiam2023gpt} or other models. (Part. \ref{sec:baselines) and \ref{sec:exp})}
        \State {\emph{writer-LLM}$\gets$ \emph{\emph{model}}}
        \Comment{Load pre-trained model for optimization, e.g., \emph{Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.1}.}
        \For{\texttt{\emph{round}} $\gets$ {1, ..., $K$}}
            \State \emph{$review \gets$ \texttt{\emph{writer-LLM}($paper$, reasons, evidence, writer)} 
            \Comment{Generate \emph{review} based on \emph{paper}, \emph{reasons} and \emph{evidence}. (Part. \ref{sec:prompt_design})}
            \State \emph{feedback}$_{\emph{r}}, \emph{suggestions} \gets$ \texttt{\emph{langauge-agent}($paper$, review, domain)} \Comment{Get \emph{feedback} from structured language agent (Part. \ref{sec:prompt_design})}
            \If{\emph{stopcriterion}}
            \State \Return $review$
            \Else
            \State \emph{writer-LLM} $\gets$ \texttt{optimize(\emph{writer-LLM}, review, feedback, suggestions)}

        \EndIf
