\subsection{Fine-tuning for Enhanced Comprehension of Scientific Domains}
\label{sec:optimization_llm}

Unlike ReviewGPT \citep{liu2023reviewergpt}, which is designed to write reviews without any optimization, and MAVer \citep{darcar2024maver}, which is solely based on prompts with a lack of optimization, \ours introduces a fine-tuning process to enhance the capability of LLMs in writing, as illustrated in \Cref{fig:overview}. This is important, as the quality of the reviews generated by LLMs depends on their ability to comprehend research papers in target domains.

However, optimizing a model's parameters across the entire model layer by layer requires substantial computational resources, especially for large language models. Recent research \citep{hu2022lora, kuzmin2024fp8quantizationpowerexponent, wang2024loragalowrankadaptationgradient} has demonstrated Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) as an effective approach for enhancing model performance while preventing catastrophic forgetting. Additionally, LoRA reduces computational requirements as it only focuses on optimizing embeddings and a low-rank weight matrix. In simple terms, LoRA incorporates a structure with low-rank matrices into regular model parameters, resulting in the formation of $M \times (4M + 2\emph{H}^2)$ total parameters, where $\emph{M}$ is the empirical parameter of LoRA and $\emph{H}$ corresponds to the number of attention heads. This structure significantly reduces computational requirements when compared to the original structure of $4M \times \emph{H}$. In this study, we utilize LoRA to adapt the base model to the specific requirements of the target writing, a crucial aspect of \ours. A detailed discussion on LoRA is provided in \Cref{app:LoRA}.
\subsection{Prompt-based Language Agent for Writing Feedback}
\label{sec:ai_reviewer}

\begin{figure}[ht!]
    \centering    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Figs/Fig2_wupreview.pdf}
    \caption{The prompt design of \ours, containing two decison modules. In this figure, we take scientific writing as an example.}
    \label{fig:prompt_wupreview}
\end{figure}

To provide comprehensive and accurate writing feedback for targeted review optimization, the \emph{language agent} should have review skills and understand the specific requirements of scientific writing. Therefore, we design the \emph{language agent} as a structured decision-making process inspired by \citet{weng-etal-2023-large}, which combines chain-of-thought (CoT) \citep{wei2022chain} reasoning and value estimation. The structured decision-making process contains two decision modules: value estimation and CoT reasoning. The value estimation decision module utilizes two sub-agents using different prompts to assess the value of the paper. It then computes the difference between the original value and the revised value to obtain a change score. The paper with higher change scores will be preferentially reviewed. The CoT decision module is then instructed to utilize the change scores and values to comprehend specific writing at a deeper level and derive specific reasoning. On the basis of reasoning, structure tools (e.g., extractive summarization \citep{collins2017supervised}) are employed to provide reasoning feedback and suggestions for specific writing. In this study, we concentrate on improving the evidence in the paper as a means of enhancing the quality of writing, and this method can be easily extended to other writing aspects. In \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview}, we present the structure of our designed prompt. Part (a) is the prompts for value estimation sub-agents, where positive and negative estimators are the prompts used to obtain two values through different perspectives. Part (b) is the prompt of the positive estimator with CoT reasoning, which is to get a more accurate reasoning result and writing feedback based on the positive value. Part (d) is the prompt of the negative estimator, which (optionally) uses a ``reference'' as an example to guide the estimation of values. Part (c) is to compute change scores. Part (e) is the prompt of structured language agents to perform reasoning and generate writing feedback accordingly. Part (f) is to obtain suggestions by providing a writing that needs to be improved. Part (g) is to require the language agent to summarize a core issue description with high quality and suggest solving methods. Specifically, we utilize the difference between the value obtained by the positive estimator (\emph{\emph{pos-cot-reasoner}} )and the value obtained by the negative estimator (\emph{\emph{neg-cot-reasoner}}) as the difference score to determine whether the obtained value is important, defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
    \emph{score} = \emph{value}_{\emph{pos-cot-reasoner}}-\emph{value}_{\emph{neg-cot-reasoner}}
\end{equation}

Part (h) of \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview} is the prompt of \ours for writing, where the LLM functions as a writer (\Cref{fig:overview}). This prompt aims to get high-quality writing for iterative optimization and provides two pieces of information to the writer: 1) the writing to be improved and 2) domain-specific concepts and concepts such as ``evidence'', which help the writer improve writing by providing domain-specific definitions and examples. 

\subsection{Multi-round Review with Iterative Optimization}
\label{sec:multi-round}

To obtain high-quality writing outputs, it is crucial to leverage automated LLM feedback \citep{d2024marg}. Inspired by the work of \citet{shinn2023reflexion}, we propose a multi-round review system based on iterative optimization with structured language agents. After the initial round of writing generation, subsequent rounds aim to improve writing quality by providing automated feedback to the reviews obtained in the previous rounds. This process is performed by an agent serving as the reviewer. In general, iterative optimization aims to achieve two goals:

\begin{figure}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Figs/Fig3_Iterative_Process.pdf}
    \caption{Illustration of the iterative process of \ours.}
    \label{fig:multi-round-illustration}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Goal 1: Improve the overall quality of the review by making the review more aligned with the target format.} 
To achieve this, we provide two iterative optimization modules: value estimation and quality assessment. The value estimation module obtains a value score for each review and is used to address \emph{the lack of understanding of writing requirements}. Specifically, value estimation language agents use \emph{change scores} to get important reviews to write and then estimate the value of the paper, similar to the process of an agent reviewer described in the previous section. The quality assessment module language agent aims to evaluate the quality of the review based on the requirements, and then provide three scores for the review based on criteria: completeness of the response section (the ``Completeness'' score), ease of understanding (the ``Clearness'' score), and soundness of the reasoning (the ``Authority'' score). The completeness criterion evaluates whether the issues raised by the reviewer have been addressed and is mandatory. The other two are optional, depending on the background of the reviewers. In cases where they disagree, either criterion can be retained, and the other will be omitted. Additionally, we also propose a method to compute scores using cMCC. The specific prompt design for quality assessment is detailed in \Cref{app:prompt}. All the gathered scores are used to compute a winrate, and the process of computing the winrate is defined as:
\begin{equation}
    \emph{winrate} = \frac{\sum_{i=\frac{1, 2, ..., n}{3}}^{n} (1 + \emph{score}_\emph{review}_i - \emph{score}_\emph{human}_i)}{\emph{len}(\emph{review})}
\end{equation}

In this process, the LLM reviewer uses a high-quality review as a reference to summarize the issues with the opponent's review and their suggested solutions. \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview} (i) and (j) present the prompts used to obtain a summarized issue description and suggested solving methods, respectively. 

\textbf{Goal 2: Help writer-LLM be more consistent with the review of the domain-specific writing requirements.} 
To achieve this, we provide two modules for iterative optimization: feedback and suggestions. The feedback process aims to provide detailed feedback on the writing improvements, and the suggested module aims to provide solutions to specific issues to guide the writer's revision. In this process, the structured language agent provides writing feedback and suggestions for improvement by referring to the higher-quality review obtained from the previous round. In \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview}, the prompts for generating feedback and suggestions are specified in \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview} (l) and (m), respectively.

