\textbf{Comparison of Different Methods}:
\Cref{tab:exp_results} shows the comparison of different methods in terms of overall quality and writing quality. The result of human review is included as a reference. \ours achieves \textbf{91\%} winrate compared to the human review, which is better than MAVer and ReviewGPT. Part. (b) and Part. (d) of \Cref{tab:exp_results} correspond to the prompt used for overall review and writing quality evaluation. The results of different metrics are detailed in \Cref{tab:coherence}. The experiments show that \ours has a winrate of \textbf{91\%} compared to the human review, which is better than MAVer, ReviewGPT and ReviewBot. Specifically, we find that: \textbf{1)} For overall quality, \ours has a higher writing score than other methods, indicating that it achieves better performance in overall quality of the writing. \ours has a KL divergence of \textbf{8.4\%} compared to human, which means that it has a higher level of human understanding in the AI review. The coherence metrics are close to those of humans, especially the coherent (max) score which is even higher than humans, indicating that there are AI reviews within 5-degree distance to human levels and no AI reviews are in the 5-degree lower position. The complete version of \Cref{tab:coherence} is shown in \Cref{app:coherence}. \textbf{2)} For writing quality, \ours has a higher writing score than other methods, which indicates that it achieves better performance in writing quality than other methods. Specifically, the AI writing style is different from that of humans, as the KL divergence is 20.6\%, which is even higher than that of MAVer. These can be attributed to the difference in the selected paper sets. MAVer utilizes a combination of similar and dissimilar papers, which leads to a smaller range of divergences, whereas our paper set primarily consists of similar papers, resulting in a larger lower bound and upper bound. The coherence metric shows that \ours has a higher quality of writing than MAVer. Additionally, the coherence metric of \ours is better than that of ReviewGPT, indicating that our method is better than ReviewGPT in the writing quality of the AI review.

\textbf{Ablation Study}: We assess the ability of \ours to generalize to unseen scientific domains and specific writing. As the ML domain is an example, we conduct the following experiments: \textbf{1)} Obtain a background based on paper. We execute this process with the training set (detailed in \Cref{app:ablation}). \textbf{2)} Fine-tuning the original paper. For a fair comparison, we fine-tune other methods on the original paper to assess their ability to generalize. The frameworks we use are: \textbf{1)} \ours (\texttt{w} = 1,050; \texttt{l} = 60; $\alpha$ = 128), \textbf{2)} \ours-w/O-Opt (\texttt{w} = 1,050; \texttt{l} = 60; $\alpha$ = 128) not conducts multi-round optimization, \textbf{3)} \ours-w/o-domain (\texttt{w} = 2,000; \texttt{l} = 120; $\alpha$ = 128) removes the domain-specific information in \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview} and \textbf{4)} \ours-w/o-evidence (\texttt{w} = 1,050; \texttt{l} = 60; $\alpha$ = 128) removes the evidence keyword in \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview}. We also evaluate two methods to assess if adding extra optimization and data or just prompt design. We use the code provided by \citet{darcar2024maver} for ReviewGPT and use their paper-rankler tool to get the reward score. We conduct $K=3$ rounds for multi-round review. Part. (e) of \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview} is the prompt for writing when $n$$=$2, $K=3$. Note that MAVer is not included due to the absence of fine-tuned code. The results are presented in \Cref{tab:ablation}. \textbf{1)} We observe that \emph{\ours} is better than MAVer and ReviewGPT. This means that optimization and prompt-based design is better than prompt-based, solely relying on the provided reward model. \textbf{2)} We find that \ours is not significantly worse than \ours-o/Opt, indicating that the optimization is sufficient. \textbf{3)} \ours-w/o-domain is worse than \ours, indicating that domain information is crucial in scientific writing.
\textbf{3)} We find that evidence is crucial for optimization and leads to better performance. 
