\section{Related Works}

\label{sec:related_works}

\textbf{Peer Reviewer-AI Interaction}: Language agents have been employed to interact with human peer reviewers in various ways. ReviewerGPT \citep{du2024llms} utilizes a reward model to optimize reviews towards predefined criteria, but it lacks access to the peer review process. \citet{jin2024agentreivewexploringpeerreview} propose ReviewBot, a dialogue-based agent focusing on key aspects of peer reviewing such as clarity and contribution to the field. Similarly, \citet{tyser2024ai} propose AutoSurvey, which assists AI reviewers in providing feedback to the reviewers in the absence of paper. These methods primarily leverage reinforcement learning and prompt design, but they do not fully integrate domain knowledge into the review process. \ours, however, introduces quality-focused fine-tuning and automated feedback to enhance the review process.

\textbf{Scientific Discovery}: The application of LLM to facilitate scientific discovery has emerged as an ongoing trend. Language agents have been employed to formalize paper facets and generate hypothesis \citep{yang2023large,wang2024autosurvey,reid2024gemini}. They have also been used to automate experiment design and data analysis, such as generating program code \citep{NEURIPS2023_91f18a12, romer2023gpt4, langley2024integrated} and accelerating materials discovery \citep{pyzer2022accelerating, merchant2023scaling}. Additionally, they assist with writing and they have been applied to the process of peer reviewing \citep{liu2023reviewergpt, jin2024agentreivew, tan2024peerreviewmultiturnlongcontext}. As the automation progresses, these methods evolve into fully autonomous research workflows and management systems \citep{li2024ai4r, hu2024ai, yakaboski2023ai, taniguchi2024collectivepredictivecodingmodel,radensky2024scideator,li2024mlrcopilotautonomousmachinelearning, baek2024researchagent}. These advancements offer insights into the potential of LLM-assisted AI scientists, but fully automated scientific discovery and personalized research workflows remain a distant ambition.

\textbf{Iterative Reasoning}: Iterative reasoning, which uses reinforcement learning with human or AI feedback to enhance reasoning abilities, has gained attention due to its effectiveness in enhancing the reasoning capabilities of LLMs. \citet{rafailov2023direct} demonstrate that LLMs can iteratively improve their performance by learning from their errors. \citet{pang2024iterative} propose a simple yet effective preference optimization method that utilizes chain-of-thought to iteratively learn from feedback. \citet{xiong2024iterativepreferencelearninghuman} propose iterative preference learning using human and AI feedback, which balances both signals. \citet{lee2024rlaifvsrlhfscaling} leverage reinforcement learning from AI feedback for reinforcement learning from human feedback. In the field of NLP, chain-of-thought reasoning has been widely utilized to enhance the reasoning abilities of language models \citep{an2023learning, wang2024loragalowrankadaptationgradient}. These methods can be adapted to fully automate the process of fine-tuning and align LLMs more closely with human preferences, thereby accelerating the adoption of AI as an effective tool for scientific discovery. These works have been used in various contexts, such as designing new AI agents \citep{Hu2024AutomatedDO}, or as a means of optimizing LLMs for specific tasks or domains \citep{yuan2024self, zhu2024safetylock}. The ability to refine their responses through iterative reasoning can also enhance the robustness of LLMs, reducing their susceptibility to hallucinations and other errors.



\section{Conclusion and Future Works}

\label{sec:conclusion}

In this paper, we introduce \ours, a framework employing language agents to automate commenting on academic papers. \ours utilizes LoRA for quality-focused fine-tuning and employs a multi-round review process for iterative optimization. Through experiments, we find that \ours achieves 91\% winrate over human reviewers in the target scientific domain, highlighting its significant potential in accelerating scientific discovery.

In future research, we aim to extend our framework to the broader application domain of natural language processing. Moreover, \ours can serve as a basis for fully autonomous scientific discovery by incorporating more automated experiment designs and data analysis. These advancements will gradually reduce the need for manual intervention in the scientific discovery process, paving the way for fully autonomous scientific discovery in the future.





\section{Disclosure}

This paper was written with the assistance of CycleResearcher, including but not limited to the introduction, related work, experimental design, and experimental results sections. A portion of the content may have been generated using large language models (LLMs). The CycleResearcher project is supported by Westlake University (WestlakeNLP).

