\title{WUPReview: A Quality-focused Writing-guided Peer Review System with Reinforcement Learning}

\begin{abstract}
We propose \ours, a writing process-guided peer review system based on language agents for scientific discovery. \ours is designed to improve the writing quality of research papers, guided by a quality-focused prompt-engineered language agent in a multi-round format. To achieve this, we first finetune an LLM to comprehend the writing styles of a specific format and concept to facilitate the review. \ours then provides automated refinement and iterative learning based on writing feedback. It achieves state-of-the-art performance in various metrics, including an average 91\% winrate against human reviews in related papers. This demonstrates the potential of \ours in automating scientific discovery, signifying a significant advancement in the application of artificial intelligence to the process of peer review.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}



\begin{figure}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Figs/Fig1_wupreview.pdf}
    \caption{The overview of our \ours (Writing-focused Peer Review) framework. \ours contains two key modules: 1) Quality-focused fine-tuning, which aims to enhance the quality of model-generated reviews, and 2) Multi-round review with iterative optimization, which aims to iteratively improve upon the previous generations and output a high-quality review.}
    \label{fig:overview}
\end{figure}

Deep learning has been applied to various scientific tasks \citep{jumper2021highly,hayes2024simulating,ai4science2023impact, merchant2023scaling,pyzer2022accelerating}. Among them, artificial intelligence (AI) assisted scientific discovery based on large language models (LLMs) has received much attention in recent years \citep{langley1987scientific,langley2024integrated}. \citet{wang2023scientific} argue that the process of scientific discovery can be autonomously accelerated and steered by AI. They propose a Scientific Discovery Engine (SDE), which uses LLMs to streamline the end-to-end scientific workflow from experimental design to obtaining results. \citet{lu2024ai} further introduce an LLM-powered autonomous agent to replace the complex, team-oriented process of scientific discovery with a simpler, faster one. These works are revolutionary yet unsupported by experimental evidence, as they do not demonstrate the capability of LLMs to replace humans in every process of science. 

In fact, peer review, which aims to evaluate the validity and advance the novelty of discoveries, is a critical part of the process of scientific discovery \citep{boughton2018research}. It remains critical in an automated and an idealized scenario because it not only provides quality assurance for the soundness of findings \citep{smith2006peer}, but also provides a basis for iterative development of materials in experimental design \citep{bengio2015scaling}. Therefore, we argue that a peer review system must be automated before the entire process of scientific discovery can be fully automated.

Recently, works have explored the automation of peer reviewing using LLMs. For example, ReviewGPT \citep{liu2023reviewergpt} represents a pilot exploration. However, its design has limitations, as it does not explicitly promote LLMs' attention to a key word like ``evidence'', and the output is sometimes unsatisfactory. ReviewBot \citep{jin2024agentreivewexploringpeerreview} is another approach introduced by \citet{jin2024agentreivew} to simulate peer review through a dialogue framework. It introduces a formalized context (paper structure, etc.) in the prompt.
But such formalization overlooks the fact that an actual human writer often modifies the structure of the paper to add evidence during the revision process. In contrast, it enforces the model to strictly adhere to the original structure. As a result, ReviewBot's performance is not ideal. MAVer \citep{darcar2024maver} introduces a reference-based approach to compare the quality of model reviews with those of humans. The study indicates that MAVer's performance is more robust and better than that of ReviewGPT \citep{liu2023reviewergpt}. Nevertheless, MAVer still lacks robustness in terms of completeness and emergence, and is lacking in comparison with human reviews.

In this study, we introduce \ours, a framework for writing-focused peer review that utilizes language agents. \ours employs a language agent to comprehend the writing styles in target scientific domains and concentrates on key concepts and concepts (e.g., ``evidence''). The quality-focused fine-tuning process enhances LLMs' comprehension of scientific domains and results in significant improvements in the quality of model-generated reviews. Additionally, \ours includes a multi-round review process for iterative improvements of writing feedback. This process involves language agents offering feedback to writer-LLMs based on generated writing outputs. The writing feedback then aims to improve specific writing and is given to the writer-LLM for revision. Through this process, the proposed \ours can achieve a 91\% winrate against human reviews in related papers.

The primary contributions of this paper are as follows: First, we introduce the \ours framework, which automates peer reviews by providing fine-tuning and automated LLM feedback based on writing outputs. Furthermore, we propose an iterative reasoning and preference optimization method to further enhance the quality of writing feedback and reviews. Second, we validate the effectiveness of \ours with comprehensive experiments across various domains and models, achieving a 91\% winrate against human reviews in related papers.


\section{The WUPReview Framework}


\subsection{Overview}
Given the paper to be reviewed, the author and their writing background, and domain-specific requirements, the goal of \ours is to write a high-quality, tailored review that critically evaluates the paper. To achieve this, \ours contains two stages: 1) Quality-focused fine-tuning, which aims to enhance the quality of model-generated reviews, and 2) Multi-round review with iterative optimization.

\Cref{fig:overview} illustrates the process flow of \ours. Step 1 of the fine-tuning stage is to tailor the LLM to the writing requirements using LoRA \citep{hu2022lora} (Low-Rank Adaptation). The optimization is then based on chain-of-thought (CoT) \citep{wei2022chain} writing outputs, using the `` évidence'' (evidence) keyword as a proxy to assess reasoning quality. Note that the evidence keyword itself is not the main focus of this paper, and other keywords can be applied. Step 2 is to provide automated refinement and iterative learning based on writing feedback. Specifically, we design prompts to control language agents, enabling them to provide feedback based on the writing outputs. Then, the writing feedback aims to improve specific writing, which is further given to the writer-LLM for revision.

In this process, the model with LoRA (the one optimized by the first step) acts as the writer-LLM, which generates the initial writing. The original model then serves as a reviewer-LLM, which generates a higher-quality writing based on the feedback from the language agent. This step is performed iteratively until convergence. The detailed prompt design is shown in \Cref{app:prompt}. The process is summarized as Algorithm \ref{alg:wupreview}:
\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\small
    \caption{\ours Framework}
    \label{alg:wupreview}
    \setlength{\itemsep}{1pt}
    \SetKwFunction{background}{background}
    \SetKwFunction{document}{document}
    \SetKwFunction{evidence}{evidence}
    \begin{algorithmic}[1]
        \Require Paper to review \emph{\emph{$paper$}}, writer \emph{\emph{name}}, and domain 
        \emph{$domain$}
        \Ensure \emph{\emph{$review$}}
        \State {\textbf{reasons, writing$_{\emph{r}} \gets$ \background($paper, domain$)} 
        \Comment{Get the \emph{scientific background} from GPT-4 \citep{achiam2023gpt} or other models. (Part. \ref{sec:baselines) and \ref{sec:exp})}
        \State {\emph{writer-LLM}$\gets$ \emph{\emph{model}}}
        \Comment{Load pre-trained model for optimization, e.g., \emph{Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.1}.}
        \For{\texttt{\emph{round}} $\gets$ {1, ..., $K$}}
            \State \emph{$review \gets$ \texttt{\emph{writer-LLM}($paper$, reasons, evidence, writer)} 
            \Comment{Generate \emph{review} based on \emph{paper}, \emph{reasons} and \emph{evidence}. (Part. \ref{sec:prompt_design})}
            \State \emph{feedback}$_{\emph{r}}, \emph{suggestions} \gets$ \texttt{\emph{langauge-agent}($paper$, review, domain)} \Comment{Get \emph{feedback} from structured language agent (Part. \ref{sec:prompt_design})}
            \If{\emph{stopcriterion}}
            \State \Return $review$
            \Else
            \State \emph{writer-LLM} $\gets$ \texttt{optimize(\emph{writer-LLM}, review, feedback, suggestions)}

        \EndIf
        \EndFor
        \State \Return $review$
    \end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Fine-tuning for Enhanced Comprehension of Scientific Domains}
\label{sec:optimization_llm}

Unlike ReviewGPT \citep{liu2023reviewergpt}, which is designed to write reviews without any optimization, and MAVer \citep{darcar2024maver}, which is solely based on prompts with a lack of optimization, \ours introduces a fine-tuning process to enhance the capability of LLMs in writing, as illustrated in \Cref{fig:overview}. This is important, as the quality of the reviews generated by LLMs depends on their ability to comprehend research papers in target domains.

However, optimizing a model's parameters across the entire model layer by layer requires substantial computational resources, especially for large language models. Recent research \citep{hu2022lora, kuzmin2024fp8quantizationpowerexponent, wang2024loragalowrankadaptationgradient} has demonstrated Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) as an effective approach for enhancing model performance while preventing catastrophic forgetting. Additionally, LoRA reduces computational requirements as it only focuses on optimizing embeddings and a low-rank weight matrix. In simple terms, LoRA incorporates a structure with low-rank matrices into regular model parameters, resulting in the formation of $M \times (4M + 2\emph{H}^2)$ total parameters, where $\emph{M}$ is the empirical parameter of LoRA and $\emph{H}$ corresponds to the number of attention heads. This structure significantly reduces computational requirements when compared to the original structure of $4M \times \emph{H}$. In this study, we utilize LoRA to adapt the base model to the specific requirements of the target writing, a crucial aspect of \ours. A detailed discussion on LoRA is provided in \Cref{app:LoRA}.
\subsection{Prompt-based Language Agent for Writing Feedback}
\label{sec:ai_reviewer}

\begin{figure}[ht!]
    \centering    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Figs/Fig2_wupreview.pdf}
    \caption{The prompt design of \ours, containing two decison modules. In this figure, we take scientific writing as an example.}
    \label{fig:prompt_wupreview}
\end{figure}

To provide comprehensive and accurate writing feedback for targeted review optimization, the \emph{language agent} should have review skills and understand the specific requirements of scientific writing. Therefore, we design the \emph{language agent} as a structured decision-making process inspired by \citet{weng-etal-2023-large}, which combines chain-of-thought (CoT) \citep{wei2022chain} reasoning and value estimation. The structured decision-making process contains two decision modules: value estimation and CoT reasoning. The value estimation decision module utilizes two sub-agents using different prompts to assess the value of the paper. It then computes the difference between the original value and the revised value to obtain a change score. The paper with higher change scores will be preferentially reviewed. The CoT decision module is then instructed to utilize the change scores and values to comprehend specific writing at a deeper level and derive specific reasoning. On the basis of reasoning, structure tools (e.g., extractive summarization \citep{collins2017supervised}) are employed to provide reasoning feedback and suggestions for specific writing. In this study, we concentrate on improving the evidence in the paper as a means of enhancing the quality of writing, and this method can be easily extended to other writing aspects. In \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview}, we present the structure of our designed prompt. Part (a) is the prompts for value estimation sub-agents, where positive and negative estimators are the prompts used to obtain two values through different perspectives. Part (b) is the prompt of the positive estimator with CoT reasoning, which is to get a more accurate reasoning result and writing feedback based on the positive value. Part (d) is the prompt of the negative estimator, which (optionally) uses a ``reference'' as an example to guide the estimation of values. Part (c) is to compute change scores. Part (e) is the prompt of structured language agents to perform reasoning and generate writing feedback accordingly. Part (f) is to obtain suggestions by providing a writing that needs to be improved. Part (g) is to require the language agent to summarize a core issue description with high quality and suggest solving methods. Specifically, we utilize the difference between the value obtained by the positive estimator (\emph{\emph{pos-cot-reasoner}} )and the value obtained by the negative estimator (\emph{\emph{neg-cot-reasoner}}) as the difference score to determine whether the obtained value is important, defined as follows:
\begin{equation}
    \emph{score} = \emph{value}_{\emph{pos-cot-reasoner}}-\emph{value}_{\emph{neg-cot-reasoner}}
\end{equation}

Part (h) of \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview} is the prompt of \ours for writing, where the LLM functions as a writer (\Cref{fig:overview}). This prompt aims to get high-quality writing for iterative optimization and provides two pieces of information to the writer: 1) the writing to be improved and 2) domain-specific concepts and concepts such as ``evidence'', which help the writer improve writing by providing domain-specific definitions and examples. 

\subsection{Multi-round Review with Iterative Optimization}
\label{sec:multi-round}

To obtain high-quality writing outputs, it is crucial to leverage automated LLM feedback \citep{d2024marg}. Inspired by the work of \citet{shinn2023reflexion}, we propose a multi-round review system based on iterative optimization with structured language agents. After the initial round of writing generation, subsequent rounds aim to improve writing quality by providing automated feedback to the reviews obtained in the previous rounds. This process is performed by an agent serving as the reviewer. In general, iterative optimization aims to achieve two goals:

\begin{figure}[t!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Figs/Fig3_Iterative_Process.pdf}
    \caption{Illustration of the iterative process of \ours.}
    \label{fig:multi-round-illustration}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Goal 1: Improve the overall quality of the review by making the review more aligned with the target format.} 
To achieve this, we provide two iterative optimization modules: value estimation and quality assessment. The value estimation module obtains a value score for each review and is used to address \emph{the lack of understanding of writing requirements}. Specifically, value estimation language agents use \emph{change scores} to get important reviews to write and then estimate the value of the paper, similar to the process of an agent reviewer described in the previous section. The quality assessment module language agent aims to evaluate the quality of the review based on the requirements, and then provide three scores for the review based on criteria: completeness of the response section (the ``Completeness'' score), ease of understanding (the ``Clearness'' score), and soundness of the reasoning (the ``Authority'' score). The completeness criterion evaluates whether the issues raised by the reviewer have been addressed and is mandatory. The other two are optional, depending on the background of the reviewers. In cases where they disagree, either criterion can be retained, and the other will be omitted. Additionally, we also propose a method to compute scores using cMCC. The specific prompt design for quality assessment is detailed in \Cref{app:prompt}. All the gathered scores are used to compute a winrate, and the process of computing the winrate is defined as:
\begin{equation}
    \emph{winrate} = \frac{\sum_{i=\frac{1, 2, ..., n}{3}}^{n} (1 + \emph{score}_\emph{review}_i - \emph{score}_\emph{human}_i)}{\emph{len}(\emph{review})}
\end{equation}

In this process, the LLM reviewer uses a high-quality review as a reference to summarize the issues with the opponent's review and their suggested solutions. \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview} (i) and (j) present the prompts used to obtain a summarized issue description and suggested solving methods, respectively. 

\textbf{Goal 2: Help writer-LLM be more consistent with the review of the domain-specific writing requirements.} 
To achieve this, we provide two modules for iterative optimization: feedback and suggestions. The feedback process aims to provide detailed feedback on the writing improvements, and the suggested module aims to provide solutions to specific issues to guide the writer's revision. In this process, the structured language agent provides writing feedback and suggestions for improvement by referring to the higher-quality review obtained from the previous round. In \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview}, the prompts for generating feedback and suggestions are specified in \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview} (l) and (m), respectively.

