In this section, we discuss related papers on LLM-based peer reviewing of research papers, categorizing them into three types of works: exploration, assistance, and automation. We show the comparison in \Cref{tab:related_work_class}. 
\textbf{Exploration}: Peer review assistance works aim to reduce the workload of reviewers. They provide feedback to peer reviewers such as \citet{wang2023autosurvey} and \citet{langley2024integrated} support for writing. They aim to assist peer reviewers in improving writing efficiency and reduce annotation effort, thus helping peer reviewers to improve writing efficiency and reduce the effort of annotation. They do not replace the review process of the reviewers. 

\textbf{Assistance}: Peer review-based exploration works do not require reviewers to provide a specific outcome, and are mainly used to explore the potential of LLM in a single attempt. For example, ReviewGPT \citep{liu2023reviewergpt} is a one-time effort to explore the automatic assistance in writing paper reviews without optimizing the model. The result of ReviewGPT is not high quality, which is mentioned in \citet{jin2024agentreivew}. The authors of ReviewBot \citep{jin2024agentreivew} propose a dialogue-based method, which introduces a formalized context (paper format) in the prompt, thus prompting the model to follow the structure strictly. Additionally, they utilize chain-of-thought prompting to perform reasoning. However, such formalization is overly strict, as a human author may modify the structure of the paper to add evidence during revision. In contrast, ReviewBot enforces the model to strictly adhere to the original structure. As a result, this over-restrictiveness may introduce limitations. For example, during iterative optimization in multi-round reviews, ReviewBot modification of the paper structure generation prompt may consistently result in non-sensical reviews. To bridge the gap between ReviewBot and human reviews, ReviewerGPT \citep{du2024llms} introduces extra optimization, which aims to align model reviews with those of humans. 
\textbf{Automation}: 
Peer review assistance works do not replace the specific outcomes of the reviewers, thus they do not fully automate the peer review. Peer review-based exploration works are primarily used to explore the potential of LLM in a single attempt, thus they are far from fully automated. The primary goal of works advancing the automation of peer review is to enhance the quality of the review by automating the review process and leveraging domain knowledge in the target paper. For example, MAVer \citep{darcar2024maver} introduces a reference-based approach, allowing the LLM to think analogically when reading papers. MAVer compares the quality of model reviews with those of humans to address the drawbacks of previous works such as ReviewGPT. It employs a reward model to estimate the quality of reviews and to choose the highest-reward paper for the next iteration. Despite its success, MAVer still lacks robustness in terms of completeness and emergence, and is lacking in comparison with human reviews. 



\section{Experiments}

\label{sec:exp}

This section first outlines the experiment settings for evaluating \ours. We then proceed to compare existing methods in terms of performance as well as different backbones. We also verify the effectiveness of fine-tuning and the multi-round optimization design. A case study is elaborated to illustrate the performance of these methods.

\subsection{Settings}\label{sec:settings}
\textbf{Training Details}: Considering the length limitations imposed by the models, we manually summarized the paper to be reviewed using the \texttt{reportlinker} \footnote{\url{https://reportlinker.
The background introduced in \Cref{sec:baselines} was used as input for the writer-LLM to generate an initial writing, which was then used by the language agent to provide writing feedback. We conduct $N$-round reviews, with each round resulting in a new writing. Part. (d) of \Cref{fig:prompt_wupreview} is the prompt for writing when $n$$>$1$.

\textbf{Datasets}: We randomly choose 200 papers from ACL conferences, and randomly choose 100 papers from ACL conferences as references in fine-tuning. We use the 100 papers fine-tuned by \citet{darcar2024maver} in the code provided by the authors. Part of the paper is omitted due to space limitations (e.g.,: ``S. Bengio, M. Hinton and A. Courtier, \emph{Deep Learning}, 2015''). For the ablation study, we utilized 100 papers in the ML domain as detailed in \citet{darcar2024maver}. We conduct an iterative review experiment using 30 papers from the remaining paper set, with each paper undergoing three rounds of review: by the human, the original \ours and the optimized \ours. 

\textbf{Evaluation Metrics}: Following \citet{liang2024can}, we first use human-AI winrates for overall quality and quality of writing. The score is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\emph{score} = \emph{LLM} + 100\emph{human}
\end{equation}
The LLM is a specific method's review and the human is the human review. The method with the higher score wins \texttt{1}/\emph{two points} and draws \texttt{0.5} point. The final result is the sum of LLM and human score. Additionally, Coherent (\emph{5\u00b0$\pm$5\u00b0}) (max), Coherent (\emph{5\u00b0$\pm$5\u00b0}) (mean), and Incoherent are used to evaluate writing quality, similar to how \citet{liu2023reviewergpt} evaluate dialogue. Coherent (max) represents the maximum score within \emph{5\u00b0} of the human score, Coherent (mean) represents the average score within \emph{5\u00b0} of the human score, and Incoherent represents reviews that are \emph{5\u00b0} lower than the human score. The lower the Incoherent score, the better the result. Specifically, incoherent means the review is totally wrong. \emph{5\u00b0} refers to the deviation from human.

