\section{Problem Formulation}

\label{sec:problem-formulation}

\paragraph{Peer reviewing process}
is a formal process aimed at collectively improving the quality of research manuscripts to help the community to grow. The program starts with a reviewer who is an expert in a specific field of knowledge (e.g., a computer science researcher ~\citep{boughton2018research, smith2006peer,NEURIPS2020_6b493230,tyser2024ai}, or AI-assisted reviewer in our work) getting assigned a manuscript to review. The specific activities of the reviewer include  prior knowledge preparation, carefully reading the manuscript and providing references, and finally, writing down a review and providing a recommendation on behalf of the program. In this process, the reviewer should perform the following roles: \textbf{expertise}, \textbf{cognitive judgment}, \textbf{consistency}, \textbf{adherence}, and be \textbf{amiliar with} the manual operation. A detailed description of these roles is provided in \cref{app:prompt-template}. 


\paragraph{AI-assisted reviewer.} We aim to employ large language models (LLMs) to assist the peer review process. Our goal with the AI-assisted reviewer is to perform the role-playing activities mentioned above and provide a similar or superior level of review quality to that of a human. To achieve this, we investigate three technical directions (see ~\Cref{fig:teaser}(b) for an overview): 1) \textbf{Fine-tuning reward models}: We fine-tune a more detailed reward model at the role-playing level to measure the quality of the AI-assisted reviewer.  2) \textbf{Iterative preference optimization}: We follow the iterative preference optimization (IPO) framework to collect reviews from both human and AI-assisted reviewers, train a reward model, and improve the AI-assisted reviewer's review quality with the fine-tuned reward model through reinforcement learning or AI-assisted preference optimization. 3) \textbf{Chain-of-though optimization}: We follow the chain-of-though prompting approaches to perform multiple rounds of revisions for both labels and texts to enhance the quality of the AI-assisted reviewer. We will detail the optimization process and prompt template in \cref{sec:review-quality-analysis}. We use the fine-tuned reward model to assess the performance of the AI-assisted reviewer in the above three optimization directions. 


\paragraph{Evaluation Metrics.} Needing to stress that the peer reviewing process is far more complex than just reviewing a single aspect of a manuscript. Thus, following existing works \citep{liu2023reviewergpt,du2024llms}, we assess the quality of a review through three aspects: \textbf{general quality}, \textbf{recommendation}, and \textbf{review}. For \textit{general review quality}, we follow ~\citet{lambert2024rewardbench} and use \texttt{fluency} (0-10) to measure the clarity, facility, and diversity of the reviews. ~\texttt{Relevance} (0-5) is to assess the degree to which the review stays within the scope of the manuscript. For \textit{recommendation quality}, we use term ``Accept/Reject" to measure if the reviewer concludes that the manuscript should be accepted or rejected. For \textit{review quality}, following ~\citet{lambert2024rewardbench}, we propose five dimensions: \texttt{Expertise} (0-10), \texttt{Cognitive Judgment} (0-10),  \texttt{Consistency}, \texttt{Duty and Ethics Awareness}, and \texttt{Familiarity}. Both \texttt{Consistency} (0-5) and \texttt{Duty and Ethics Awareness} (0-5) are rated are the degree to which the review stays within an appropriate and duty-aware scope of the manuscript, while both \texttt{Expertise} and \texttt{Cognitive Judgment} (0-10) are rated on how well-informed the review of the subject matter, how critical, how deep and specific the analysis is, and how substantive the review is. \texttt{Familiarity} (0-5) is scored based on the difficulty of using the AI-assisted reviewer. 
These dimensions are rated on a scale of 0-5, with higher scores indicating better performance. 
To obtain the human score at the above seven dimensions, we recruit three human scientists to read and score the manuscript based on their own opinions and criteria. 
More details are in~\cref{app:prompt-template} and ~\cref{app:annotation}.




\section{\method{}

\label{sec:review-method}

In this section, we detail our \method{} system to optimize an AI-assisted reviewer LLM to assist the full peer reviewing process. We first provide an overview of the \method{} system and then each component. 
\subsection{Overview of \method{}} 
\label{sec:method-overview}
Overall, the \method{} system is aimed to perform the full peer reviewing process with LLMs to emulate the role-playing capabilities of human reviewers. The underlying research question we seek to answer here is \textit{what neural AI-assisted reviewer generates better human-level reviews?} 
To answer this question, we conduct a comprehensive quality analysis to assess the effectiveness and efficiency of \method{} in \cref{sec:review-quality-analysis}.
Specifically, we recruit three experts to review over 800 double-blind reviewed manuscripts (over 800 different NLP research papers) released in January 2024 from ACL Anthology, and collect three different reviews for each manuscript. Among them, some are reviewed by human, and some are reviewed by our AI-assisted reviewer. Then, we fine-tune a detailed reward model at the role-playing level to measure the quality of the AI-assisted reviewer. 

For IPO, we first alternate three steps (see \cref{fig:method}(a) for an overview): 1) we collect human and AI-assisted reviews at step N to form a new pool of reviews, then sample different pairs of reviews based on their different labels (e.g., 0-5 scores at different review quality dimensions); 2) we train a reward model at step N with a new mix of data; 3) we perform a reinforcement learning or preference optimization with the fine-tuned reward model. Then, we perform chain-of-though optimizations to enhance the quality of the AI-assisted reviewer. We propose a prompt template based on the chain-of-though prompting~\citep{wei2022chain, weng-etal-2023-large, zhu2024locking} to perform multi-round  revisions for both labels and texts to enhance the quality of AI-assisted reviewer outputs. We will detail the prompt template and the chain-of-though optimization process in \cref{sec:review-quality-analysis}.
\subsection{Reward Model Fine-tuning}
\label{sec:reward-model}
Following existing works~\citep{lambert2024rewardbench}, we first collect human and AI-assisted reviews to fine-tune a reward model. Then, we follow a multi-aspect preference optimization framework~\citep{jiang2023mistral} to fine-tune the reward model at the role-playing level. NeurIPS is a very special dataset with over 800 manuscripts released in January 2024 from ACL Anthology. We collected three different reviews from three human scientists for each of them. Meanwhile, we collected three different AI-assisted reviews generated by \texttt{gpt-4} and provided all of them to the three scientists. Then the three scientists scored all of these reviews based on their own opinions and criteria. We mixed all these different reviews based on different labels at different dimensions and fine-tuned a \texttt{LLaMA-3-8B-Instruct}~\footnote{https://huggingface.co/Meta-Llama/Llama-3-8B} reward model with 5000 steps. More details are in \cref{app:reward-model}. 
\subsection{Iterative Preference Optimization}
\label{sec:iterative-preference-optimization}

\paragraph{Iterative preference optimization (IPO).}
To further enhance the role-playing capabilities of the reviewer LLM with the fine-tuned reward model, we follow the IPO framework~\citep{pang2024iterative} to collect reviews from both human and AI-assisted reviewers, train a reward model with mixed data, and improve the AI-assisted reviewer's quality with the reward model. We first alternate three steps: 1) we collect human and AI-assisted reviews at step N to form a new pool of reviews, then sample different pairs of reviews based on their different labels; 2) we train a reward model at step N with a new mix of data; 3) we perform reinforcement learning or preference optimization with the fine-tuned reward model. Summarizing the process of IPO in \cref{fig:method}(a), we first collect three different reviews from different reviewers (e.g., \texttt{gpt-4}, \texttt{Llama-3-70B-Instruct}, and \method{}) for the same manuscript released in NeurIPS 2024 Anthology. Among them, some reviews are scored by humans based on seven review quality dimensions in a scale of 0-10. Then, we perform N+1 round of preference optimization with the seven fine-grained dimensions as the preferences. We fine-tuned the reward model at each round and performed reinforcement learning or preference learning with it. We performed reinforcement learning with AI feedback~\citep{lee2024rlaifvsrlhfscaling, yuan2024self} or AI-assisted preference optimization~\citep{hu2024automated} with different base LLMs in the AI-assisted reviewer role-playing chain-of-though processes. We follow SRE~\citep{yuan2024self} as our base LLM and then optimize it with SOTA methods (e.g., PPO) with AI feedback, or SRE + SOTA methods (e.g., COT + SOTA methods) with AI-assisted methods. We compare different AI-assisted preference learning methods (e.g., PPO, SOTA, and SOTA with AI feedback in \cref{tab:rpo-main-text}). We in detail describe the AI-assisted preference optimization in \cref{app:ai-assisted-preference-optimization}.

\paragraph{Reinforcement Learning with AI Feedback~\citep{lee2024rlaifvsrlhfscaling,rafailov2023direct}.}
We follow iterative preference optimization (IPO)~\citep{pang2024iterative} with AI feedback~\citep{lee2024rlaifvsrlhfscaling} to further optimize the reviewer LLM. Specifically, we alternative between (1) review collection, (2) training the explicit reward model, and (3) learning from AI feedback. In this process, we iteratively perform N rounds of feedback-dataset collection, reward-model training, and reinforcement learning with AI feedback. More details for the reward model architecture, prompt, and training process are in \cref{app:reward-model}. In the review collection step, we collect 1,000 reviews from both human and AI-assisted reviewers. We use the 0-10 scale for general quality assessment (e.g., cognitive judgment, expertise), and the 0-5 scale for recommendation quality and review quality at other aspects. Then, we use both human scores and AI-assisted reviewer AI scores to form different pairs based on different aspects. In the reward-model training step, we train the two models with different data settings: 1) AI-assisted reviewer (AI vs. AI) setting, 2) human and AI-assisted reviewer (Human vs. AI) setting, and 3) human and human scientists (Human vs. Human) setting. In the AI feedback step, we perform PPO algorithm~\citep{schulman_2017} with each reward model, respectively.  We summarize the process of the reinforcement learning from AI feedback in \cref{app:rlhf}. As different data settings lead to different human and AI-assisted reviewer performance in different aspects in \cref{tab:data_setting}, we use three data settings for three different aspects. Besides, we use three off-the-shelf AI-assisted reviewers to perform iterative preference optimization. Specifically, we use \texttt{gpt-4} and \texttt{Llama-3-70B-Instruct}, as well as the optimized \method{} to perform iterative preference optimization. We provide more details for the AI-assisted reviewers in \cref{app:ai-assisted-reviewer}.

\subsection{Chain-of-though Optimization}
\label{sec:review-quality-analysis}

To further enhance the quality of AI-assisted reviewer outputs, we follow the chain-of-though prompting approaches~\citep{wei2022chain, weng-etal-2023-large, zhu2024locking} to perform multiple rounds of revisions for both ratings and texts to enhance the quality of the AI-assisted reviewer. We use the fine-tuned reward model to assess the performance of AI-assisted reviewer in the chain-of-though optimization process.
\paragraph{Prompt template for chain-of-though optimization.} Based on the prompt template provided in \cref{fig:prompt-template}, we summarize three different aspects of the chain-of-though optimization process into the following steps: 1) \textbf{Manual Optimization}: We optimize the initial ratings and reviews based on the outputs of the fine-tuned reward models, and manually check and revise the results to meet the requirements of fluency, relevance, and accuracy for recommendations; 2) \textbf{Automatic Optimization}: We develop a new prompt template for chain-of-though optimization shown in \cref{fig:prompt-template} and automatically revise the results based on the outputs of iterative reasoning and fine-tuned reward models; and 3) \textbf{Reward Model Based Optimization}: We assess the quality of the AI-assisted reviewer using a fine-tuned reward model and perform multiple rounds of revisions based on the result of the assessment. Specifically, we take the initial ratings (0-10 scale) and reviews as the input, and perform two steps reasoning again and again, and then output the optimized scale and review text. We test the many-round iterations to get the final output. The prompt template of chain-of-though optimization is shown in \Cref{fig:prompt-template}.



