\section{Experiments}

\label{sec:experiments}

We conduct extensive experiments to assess the general and review quality of \method{} and AI-assisted reviewers. Specifically, we focus on enhancing the role-playing capabilities of the reviewer LLM with the fine-tuned reward model, and we conduct a comprehensive quality analysis to assess the effectiveness and efficiency of \method{} in \cref{sec:review-quality-analysis}.

\subsection{Setup}

\paragraph{AI-assisted Reviewer.} We use the following AI-assisted reviewer to conduct quality analysis: \textbf{1) GPT-4 Turbo}: we use \texttt{gpt-4-turbo} to assist the peer reviewing process with the prompt provided in \cref{fig:prompt-template}. We conduct five-iteration scaling~\citep{huang2024mlagentbench} with temperature set to 0.7 to scale up outputs to five iterations; \textbf{2) OpenAI GPT-4}: we use \texttt{OpenAI GPT-4} to assist the peer reviewing process with the prompt provided in \cref{fig:prompt-template}. We conduct five-iteration scaling with temperature set to 0.7 to scale up outputs to five iterations; \textbf{3) GPT-4}: we use \texttt{GPT-4} to assist the peer reviewing process with the prompt provided in \cref{fig:prompt-template}. We conduct five-iteration scaling with temperature set to 0.7 to scale up outputs to five iterations; \textbf{4) Llama-3-70B-Instruct}: we use \texttt{Llama-3-70B-Instruct} to assist the peer reviewing process with the prompt provided in \cref{fig:prompt-template}. We conduct five-iteration scaling with temperature set to 0.7 to scale up outputs to five iterations; \textbf{5) \method{}}: we use \method{} to assist the peer reviewing process with the prompt provided in \cref{fig:prompt-template}. We conduct five-iteration scaling with temperature set to 0.7 to scale up outputs to five iterations. We provide more details for the AI-assisted reviewers in \cref{app:ai-assisted-reviewer}. We also use \texttt{Llama-3-8B-Instruct} and \texttt{Qwen2-72B-The-Self-Review} as the base LLM for the AI-assisted reviewer. Following \citet{Hu2024AutomatedDO}, we perform automated agent design for the AI-assisted reviewer. Specifically, we select the best-performing AI-assisted reviewer from over 60,000 AI-assisted reviewer configurations in terms of a reward signal as the \method{} to conduct quality analysis. We provide more details on the automated agent design in \cref{app:agent-design}.


\paragraph{Baselines.} We use the following baselines in the peer reviewing process: \textbf{1) Human}: we recruit three human scientists for quality analysis. They are all from the dual-code institution. They are all novel to the specifically manuscripts used in our work. They are all with experience in peer reviewing; \textbf{2) Author-ASSIST}~\citep{li2024ai4r}: we use the following two sets of prompts provided by ~\texttt{OpenAI GPT-4} and \texttt{gpt-4-turbo} to help the authors check and revise their manuscript; \textbf{3) Auto-ASSIST}~\citep{ai4r}: we use the following two sets of prompts provided by ~\texttt{OpenAI GPT-4} and \texttt{gpt-4-turbo} to check and revise  the manuscript without inputs of the authors; \textbf{4) Author-ASSIST-revised}: we use the same prompt as the \texttt{Auto-ASSIST} to help the authors check and revise their manuscript; \textbf{5) ReviewGPT~\citep{liu2023reviewergpt}}: we use the prompt provided by ReviewGPT to generate reviews; \textbf{6) AutoReview~\citep{du2024llms}}: we use the prompt provided by AutoReview to generate reviews. All reviews are generated by \texttt{OpenAI GPT-4-turbo}, and all other reviews are generated by \texttt{gpt-4-turbo}. We detail the prompt for the baselines in \cref{app:prompt}. We use a scaled prompt to scale up outputs to five iterations. We use the prompt provided by ~\texttt{OpenAI GPT-4} and \texttt{gpt-4-turbo} to scale up the outputs to five iterations. We scale five times for each prompt. We use greedy decoding for the baseline methods. We calculate the majority vote of the three different reviews based on the five outputs for each manuscript. We use majority vote as the final recommendation. We calculate the familiarity score based on the five different outputs for each manuscript. We provide more details about the baselines in \cref{app:baseline}.


