\title{Towards Full AI-assisted Peer Review: Scientific Peer Review Assisted by AI Reviewers with Review Quality Analysis}

\begin{abstract}


AI-assisted scientific-peer reviewing is groundbreaking and meaningful yet rarely explored. To address this challenge, we developed \method{}: an AI-assisted reviewer LLM that can perform full peer review of scientific manuscripts through role-playing and a multi-round iterative refinement approach. We fine-tuned reward models, applied iterative preference optimization, and chain-of-though optimizations to optimize \method{}. To assess its effectiveness and efficiency, we conducted a comprehensive quality analysis of reviews generated by human and LLM-assisted reviewers through three dimensions: \textit{general quality assessment}, \textit{recommendation quality}, and \textit{review quality}. Extensive results show that \method{} outperforms human reviews and OpenAI GPT-4 Turbo in general and quality assessment, outperforms Author-ASSIST-revised in recommendations, and performs competitively with Author-ASSIST and Author-ASSIST-revisd in review quality, demonstrating the effectiveness and efficiency of our approach.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}



\label{sec:intro}
Powerful large language models~\citep{achiam2023gpt, reid2024gemini, qwen2.5} have revolutionized scientific machine learning~\citep{huang2024mlagentbench}, and broad scientific research domains~\citep{hayes2024simulating, romera2024mathematical, jumper2021highly, merchant2023scaling, pyzer2022accelerating, wang2023scientific, langley2024integrated, langley1987scientific, wang2023scimon, yakaboski2023ai, li2024ai4r, ai4science2023impact, buchanan1981dendral, taniguchi2024collective}. 
This results in a significant transformative impact on the structure and speed of scientific knowledge discovery~\citep{liang2024mapping}.
Among the sub-tasks to facilitate scientific discoveries, \textit{scientific peer reviewing} assists in \textit{ideation, hypothesis formulation, hypothesis refinement, quality assurance}, and \textit{improving the cooperation and collaboration among researchers}~\citep{boughton2018research, smith2006peer}. 

Recently, LLM-assisted peer reviewing (e.g., ReviewGPT~\citep{liu2023reviewergpt}, AutoReview~\citep{du2024llms}, RewardBench~\citep{lambert2024rewardbench}) has gained attention and shown remarkable capabilities in various aspects of the peer review procedure.
Specifically, ReviewGPT and AutoReview help reviewers compile references and write reviews for experimental results manuscripts and conference-quality manuscripts without human intervention.
RewardBench proposes a new metric to measure the quality of LLM-assisted reviews from three aspects: reviewer expertise, review quality, and recommendation correctness. 
These works have significantly advanced the field of AI-assisted scientific peer reviewing, made the peer reviewing process more efficient, and assisted peer reviewers.


However, to the best of our knowledge, no existing works have offered a comprehensive model capable of scientific peer reviewing through the entire process.
In fact, full process of  peer reviewing 
requires the following main role-playing abilities: \textbf{expertise} in the field of manuscript content, \textbf{cognitive judgment} capabilities, \textbf{consistency} with  review quality assessment criteria, \textbf{adherence to work duty ethics}, and \textbf{familiarity with}  manual operation requirements. 
Existing methods mostly address specific aspects or sub-tasks of peer reviewing. For example, ReviewGPT and AutoReview help reviewers compile references and write reviews, but their outputs will be revised by humans before returning to the authors, which fails to integrate some inherent role-playing requirements. RewardBench evaluates the degree of LLM-assisted reviews and reviews the quality of human reviews, but it is far from offering a full process solution.
As shown in \Cref{fig:teaser}(a),  the full peer reviewing process involves five main steps and five different roles. Specifically, the reviewer prepares prior knowledge, then reads the manuscript and provides references as thinking notes, and finally, writes reviews for the manuscript by playing one of the five roles. In this process, the reviewer is required to have a high \textit{expertise} of the manuscript content, have a \textit{cognitive judgment} capability to extract key information from the manuscript (e.g., if the content of the paper is groundbreaking), have a \textit{consistency} with review quality assessment criteria  (e.g., completeness and relevance), and so on. 
For a detailed description of these roles, please refer to \cref{app:prompt-template}. 
In summary, no existing works have offered a comprehensive model capable of full peer reviewing that involves these role-playing aspects.

