    \includegraphics[width=0.98\linewidth]{figures/teaser.pdf}
    \caption{\textbf{(a)} The peer review process. Five main steps are (\romannumeral1)  review preparation, (\romannumeral2)  reading the manuscript and providing references, (\romannumeral3)  writing the review based on roles, (\romannumeral4)  summarizing the review results based on the recommendations, and (\romannumeral5)  making decisions. Five main roles involved are Expertise, Cognitive Judgment, Consistency with review quality assessment criteria, Adherence to work duty ethics, Familiarity with manual operation. \textbf{(b)} \method{} optimizes the review generation process with fine-tuning reward models,  iterative preference optimization, and chain-of-though optimizations.}
    \label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure}

In this work, we introduce \method{}, an AI-assisted reviewer LLM (Large Language Model) aimed to perform the full peer reviewing process with LLMs to emulate the role-playing capabilities of human reviewers. The underlying research question we seek to answer is \textit{can neural AI-assisted reviewer outperform human reviewers in different quality dimensions?}
To achieve our objective, we voyager AI-assisted reviewer along three different technical optimization directions, including (\romannumeral1) \textbf{fine-tuning reward models}, (\romannumeral2) \textbf{iterative preference optimization}, and (\romannumeral3) \textbf{chain-of-though optimization}. 
For \textit{reward model fine-tuning}, we have fine-tuned a more fine-grained reward model with both cognitive judgment and expertise role-playing capabilities. 
For \textit{iterative preference optimization}, we follow the IPO framework~\citep{pang2024iterative}, which involves collecting reviews from both human and AI-assisted reviewers, then training a reward model with mixed data, and finally improving the quality of AI-assisted reviewer with the  learned reward model through reinforcement learning from AI feedback~\citep{lee2024rlaifvsrlhfscaling, yuan2024self} or  AI-assisted preference optimization~\citep{hu2024automated}.
For \textit{chain-of-though optimization}, we follow chain-of-though prompting approaches~\citep{wei2022chain, weng-etal-2023-large} to perform multiple rounds of revisions for both labels and texts to enhance the quality of LLM-assisted reviewer outputs. 
To assess the review quality of \method{}, we conduct a comprehensive quality analysis through three different dimensions: (\romannumeral1) \textit{general quality analysis}, (\romannumeral2) \textit{recommendation quality analysis}, and (\romannumeral3) \textit{review quality analysis}. 
For \textit{general quality analysis}, we aim to assess the fluency and relevance of the returned reviews since these are the fundamental requirements for reviews. 
For \textit{recommendation quality analysis}, we aim to assess the accuracy of the returned recommendations since they are crucial and determine the next procedure of the peer reviewing process.
For \textit{review quality analysis}, we aim to assess the extent to which the returned reviews fulfill the five main roles of a reviewer (i.e., \textit{expertise}, \textit{cognitive judgment},  \textit{consistency with review quality assessment criteria}, \textit{adherence to duty ethics}, and \textit{operational familiarity}) during the review process. 
We summarize our contributions as follows:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,parsep=0pt, partopsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]
    \item We introduce an AI-assisted reviewer LLM (\method{}) aimed to perform the full peer reviewing process with LLMs to emulate the role-playing capabilities of human reviewers. 
    \item We conduct a comprehensive quality analysis through three dimensions: \textit{general quality assessment}, \textit{recommendation quality}, and \textit{review quality} to assess the effectiveness and efficiency of \method{}. 
    \item Extensive results show that \method{} outperforms human reviews and OpenAI GPT-4 Turbo in general and quality assessment, outperforms Author-ASSIST-revised in recommendations, and performs competitively with Author-ASSIST and Author-ASSIST-revisd in review quality, demonstrating the effectiveness and efficiency of our approach. 
\end{itemize}


