\section{Related Work}

\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{LLM-assisted Reviewing.} 
LLM-assisted reviewing has garnered significant attention recently. 
The authors of ReviewGPT \citep{liu2023reviewergpt} and AutoReview \citep{du2024llms} have shown the potential of LLM-assisted peer reviewing.
RewardBench \citep{lambert2024rewardbench} proposes a new metric to evaluate the auto-reviewing performance of GPT-4 and GPT-4V. ReViewGPT2 \citep{jin2024agentrevisevolving} proposes three stages to perform LLM-assisted reviewing: writing, revising, and summarizing reviews. AutoReview \citep{du2024llms} has shown that auto-review can be less tedious and efficient. ReViewGPT3 \citep{li2024mlrcopilotautonomousmachinelearning} shows that ReviewsGPT3 can do autonomous machine learning research.
Besides, \citet{taniguchi2024collectivepredictivecodingmodel} have proposed a formalized model of scientific peer reviewing process. 
The work is very close to our work since it has proposed a formalized model of scientific peer reviewing process. Our work is distinguished because we provide a comprehensive quality analysis of a full-peer reviewing process and have developed a full-peer reviewing system with LLM, which shows our work is closer to applying and practical.

\paragraph{Scientific Discovery with AI.} For decades, researchers have explored the use of computational methods to assist scientific discovery and knowledge development. Since the late 1970s, systems like EURISKO \citep{lenat1977automated}, which aims to accelerate the accumulation of scientific knowledge by proposing new methodologies for automated theory development and which incorporates artificial intelligence components, and  MetaMAMA \citep{hutter2001towards} which models scientific discovery as sequential probability computation, have laid the groundwork for AI-assisted scientific research. Recent advancements in natural language processing and deep learning \citep{goodfellow2016deep,Bengio+chapter2007,NEURIPS2020_6b493230,Hinton06,lee2024rlaifvsrlhfscaling,reid2024gemini,achiam2023gpt} have led to the development of sophisticated AI tools capable of understanding and generating sophisticated scientific literature \citep{collins2017supervised,wang2023scientific}. These tools have shown potential in aiding the understanding of complex scientific knowledge within different fields \citep{langley2024integrated,langley1987scientific,yang2023large}. LLMs have particularly shown promise in generating high-quality surveys \citep{wang2024autosurvey}, new ideas brainstorming \citep{li2024mlrcopilotautonomousmachinelearning}, and solving scientific problems \citep{jumper2021highly,merchants2023scaling,pyzer2022accelerating,hayes2024simulating,wang2023scimon,huang2024mlagentbench,langley2024integrated,langley1987scientific}. Recently, AI-assisted scientific writing tools have emerged, such as AI4Science \citep{ai4r}, which aid in accelerating the production of literary works, experimental designs, and writing \citep{lu2024ai,liu2024towards}. These tools have shown potential in facilitating the collaboration of researchers in peer reviewing \citep{li2024ai4r,yakaboski2023ai,li2024ai4r,li2024marg}.

